WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:03.334 --> 00:00:06.150
- So can I call the board to order

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:09.300
and just welcome everybody.

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.760
Let me just start with the apologies.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:14.190
So we've got apologies today

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:17.550
from Mary Watkins and from Andrew Morris

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:20.580
and actually Jeremy
Townsend and Robert Lechler,

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:23.730
who are with us this morning
in the private board session

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:27.540
are unable to join us this afternoon.

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:30.990
Can I ask members to declare
any additional conflicts

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:33.540
of interest that may have
arisen since we last met?

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:35.490
Are there any declarations of interest?

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:38.219
Thank you.

00:00:38.219 --> 00:00:41.023
And then can I ask for the
board's approval to the minutes

00:00:41.023 --> 00:00:43.833
of the meeting we held
on the 1st of February?

00:00:44.741 --> 00:00:45.840
- Yes.
- Okay.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:47.010
Okay, so we'll adopt the minutes.

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:47.940
Thank you.

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:48.773
Thank you very much.

00:00:48.773 --> 00:00:53.070
Let me just give a few comments
by way of chair's update

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:57.093
before I hand over to the chief executive.

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:00.990
I mean, clearly since we last
met seven weeks or so ago,

00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:02.940
we've had the budget,

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:04.980
and very recently, really very recently,

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:07.650
clearly, we've now
issued planning guidance

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:08.613
within that budget.

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:14.400
Clearly was a very helpful
committed investment

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:15.870
coming from the government,

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:20.625
from the chancellor attaching
to technology 3.4 billion.

00:01:20.625 --> 00:01:22.320
And that is a really welcome,

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:27.320
I think, injection into our
technology investment portfolio,

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:32.640
which will help us, I think,
not just on things like the app

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:34.770
and we've talked about that this morning,

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:37.020
but also in terms of
efficiency and productivity

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:39.570
and investment in underlying systems.

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:41.553
So I mean, really good.

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:46.770
We also had yesterday the
attitudes survey published

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:50.283
and I'd like to talk
about that in a moment.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:54.190
Can I just share some of
the visits and the events

00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:58.200
and meetings that I've engaged
upon since we last met.

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:00.750
So I did a very good actually

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:03.270
ambulance shift with the
London Ambulance Service

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:05.670
and I'm very grateful to them actually

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:08.280
for the whole day actually.

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:09.870
So there was quite a lot of time actually

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:12.090
spent in the centre and in the call centre

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:14.130
and listening into calls,

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:18.693
but also actually going out
on a shift, which again,

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:22.170
and actually Chris Hopson
actually in an interview

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:24.510
a couple of mornings ago
or yesterday morning,

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:26.850
referenced a very similar experience.

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:31.230
Whenever I do go out
with an ambulance crew,

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:33.900
it is both humbling but
also just a remarkable,

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:36.240
it's humbling for me to see,

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:37.980
but it's a remarkable experience to see

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:39.960
how well our ambulance crews engage

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:40.980
in really quite difficult,

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:44.310
obviously, quite difficult interventions.

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:46.800
And they do that with sort of courage

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:50.310
and passion and humour sometimes,

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:53.460
but actually just their
sheer professionalism.

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:57.760
And it's always a really rather
remarkable reminder to me

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:00.930
of the privilege of this job actually

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:04.293
to engage with the frontline
and to see them in action.

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:08.070
I had a very good visit
to the Royal Marsden

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:10.050
focused predominantly on genomics.

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:13.803
And that was excellent to look
at things like Liquid Biopsy,

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:17.430
which was again really
interested to see that

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:22.260
and the level of research they
have underway in that trust.

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:23.490
And last night at dinner, of course,

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:25.740
we had the chair of the Royal
Marsden join us actually.

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:29.935
And that was a good conversation
that was held by him

00:03:29.935 --> 00:03:33.240
and other guests with the board.

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:35.350
I visited the Leeds Teaching Hospital

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:41.190
where I went and saw the pressures on A&amp;E

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:43.680
also saw the chemotherapy unit

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:44.880
and had the opportunity to engage

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:47.200
with a number of patients actually

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:50.850
undertaking or undergoing chemotherapy.

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:52.860
I went to an innovation popup

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:53.970
and that was really quite interesting

00:03:53.970 --> 00:03:55.980
to see actually MedTech in action

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:58.710
and actually lots of small
private businesses suggesting

00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:03.710
and creating potential MedTech
interventions for adoption.

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:09.310
And then I went over and met
with the West Yorkshire ICB

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:15.117
hosted by Cathy Elliott
the CEO of the ICB there.

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:19.679
And we had a primary care
round table with system leaders

00:04:19.679 --> 00:04:24.420
and community partnership volunteers.

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:27.840
And actually a really, very,
very good conversation.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:30.390
A lot of pressure on that system,

00:04:30.390 --> 00:04:33.840
but actually, again, it's
always rather remarkable for me

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:35.970
and I see the enthusiasm
of the frontline engaging

00:04:35.970 --> 00:04:39.720
with really, really testing challenges.

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:41.940
In events Jacqui is here.

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:45.520
We had a very good
suppliers forum in Leeds

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:48.390
with Jacqui bringing together

00:04:48.390 --> 00:04:50.850
our leading suppliers to the NHS,

00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:53.190
had again the privilege
of being able to make

00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:55.160
the opening presentation to that.

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:58.950
It was a really good engagement,
a really good conference.

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:00.357
And actually in particular talking about

00:05:00.357 --> 00:05:02.640
the new procurement framework,

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:05.520
which comes into play I think
in the autumn of this year.

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:08.220
And actually, it's a very radical,

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:09.840
a very significant increase

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:12.210
and improvement in the
way that we will be able

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:16.230
to give effect to better procurement.

00:05:16.230 --> 00:05:17.940
We had our first Chairs Event

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:20.640
for ICB chairs and trust chairs,

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:22.440
and we brought leaders
from across the system.

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:26.280
We had sort of an over 90% turnout rate,

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:27.690
which was really, really good.

00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:31.470
And we were able to discuss in some detail

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:34.980
and do breakout groups around
challenges of system working,

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:36.750
but also actually what had been actually

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:40.110
a really quite effective
management of winter,

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:42.720
which was part of that success

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:46.713
coming actually from the
success of good systems working.

00:05:47.850 --> 00:05:50.610
With Amanda and with Simon Wessely.

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:51.990
We went and had dinner

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:54.030
with the Royal College of Psychiatrists,

00:05:54.030 --> 00:05:57.600
again, that was very generous
and kind of them to host us

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:00.270
and we had a really, I think,
good and strong conversation

00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:02.280
around what we're seeing in mental health.

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:03.450
And today, we've got a paper actually

00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:07.530
on ADHD coming later on to this board.

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:11.673
But again, a very rich conversation
with the Royal College.

00:06:12.570 --> 00:06:15.210
And I also attended as with Amanda,

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:18.420
the NHS assembly about 10 days or so ago,

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:21.570
where we each did separate
Q&amp;As and discussion,

00:06:21.570 --> 00:06:24.600
and particularly actually just focus upon

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:25.740
how we come through winter,

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:27.390
the steps that we've
taken through the winter

00:06:27.390 --> 00:06:30.000
to get to the levels of
performance that we've seen.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:32.850
And then looking forward
into current levels,

00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:34.800
levels of challenge.

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:36.870
They had a very good and
separate conversation

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:41.340
around patient safety and also
separately around innovation,

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:44.400
again, across the health service.

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:45.873
In terms about the meetings,

00:06:46.950 --> 00:06:51.950
yesterday with Vin and
with John Quinn, our CIO,

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:53.520
we were doing interviews

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:58.520
for the new chair of the cyber
security and risk committee

00:06:59.250 --> 00:07:01.227
and we saw a number of candidates,
good candidates actually,

00:07:01.227 --> 00:07:03.120
and I think we will be able to make

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:05.073
a good and strong appointment.

00:07:06.090 --> 00:07:08.790
We had the board deep
dive to remind you on AI,

00:07:08.790 --> 00:07:10.530
and that was again a very good day

00:07:10.530 --> 00:07:12.693
with some very good external speakers,

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:16.680
really knowledgeable in the
space, operating in the space.

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:18.120
And actually it does have the ability

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:20.430
to offer radical transformation

00:07:20.430 --> 00:07:22.080
actually in many aspects of our lives,

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:26.460
and potentially clearly
also to the health service.

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:28.361
We've done two stakeholder breakfasts

00:07:28.361 --> 00:07:31.750
on the topic of capital and the estate

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:35.093
chaired by Chris Hopson,

00:07:35.093 --> 00:07:36.690
and actually that brings people

00:07:36.690 --> 00:07:39.090
from across the various systems together.

00:07:39.090 --> 00:07:40.770
And we had, again, very good conversations

00:07:40.770 --> 00:07:42.810
about challenges of capital.

00:07:42.810 --> 00:07:45.540
And the second one in
particular focused more on

00:07:45.540 --> 00:07:48.990
primary care estate and
actually what the options

00:07:48.990 --> 00:07:53.990
and what the opportunities
are to engage with that topic.

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:57.000
I've chaired an NHS
England Genomics Board.

00:07:57.000 --> 00:07:59.280
Again, it's the second of those boards

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:03.390
in a reconstituted board.

00:08:03.390 --> 00:08:05.850
And again, we had very
good conversations there

00:08:05.850 --> 00:08:08.040
predominantly around data,

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:13.040
around genomic sequencing
backlog and on cancer actually.

00:08:14.020 --> 00:08:19.020
So it focus actually on
genomics treatments for cancer.

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:22.950
I'd just like to thank then the board

00:08:22.950 --> 00:08:24.780
because actually since we last met,

00:08:24.780 --> 00:08:26.190
all of the committees have operated,

00:08:26.190 --> 00:08:29.957
it's often not understood
that below the board

00:08:29.957 --> 00:08:32.280
that's here evident today,

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:33.600
we have a range of committees

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:35.130
and these committees work hugely hard.

00:08:35.130 --> 00:08:39.600
If we think that we have brought
together NHS Improvement,

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:42.927
NHS Digital, NHSX, and HEE.

00:08:42.927 --> 00:08:44.310
And actually I think David,

00:08:44.310 --> 00:08:45.980
you reminded us this morning actually,

00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:48.750
if we think about the HEE board,

00:08:48.750 --> 00:08:51.030
it used to actually run
essentially 40 meetings

00:08:51.030 --> 00:08:53.850
in a year to cover the
breadth of the agenda.

00:08:53.850 --> 00:08:56.550
And now of course that is
now predominantly covered

00:08:56.550 --> 00:08:58.590
through one of our committees,

00:08:58.590 --> 00:09:03.590
which is the Workforce Talent
and Education Committee.

00:09:03.900 --> 00:09:06.240
But so the Audit and
Risk Committee has met,

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:09.030
the Quality Committee has met twice.

00:09:09.030 --> 00:09:12.570
The Workforce Talent and
Education Committee has met,

00:09:12.570 --> 00:09:15.237
Data Digital and Technology
Committee has met,

00:09:15.237 --> 00:09:18.540
the CFO Advisory group has also met.

00:09:18.540 --> 00:09:20.940
And then, Wol, under your chairmanship,

00:09:20.940 --> 00:09:22.830
we've had I think
probably the penultimate,

00:09:22.830 --> 00:09:27.830
the penultimate of the new
NHS England committees,

00:09:27.930 --> 00:09:28.980
which, of course, is the committee

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:33.060
that has overseen the merger
of these five entities

00:09:33.060 --> 00:09:34.980
and actually bringing
together the workforce

00:09:34.980 --> 00:09:37.500
of the 24,000 workforce originally

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:39.960
and the reduction in that workforce

00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:43.800
by around 35, 36%.

00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:44.633
But more importantly,

00:09:44.633 --> 00:09:47.760
making sure that the head
office organisations now

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:50.400
combined as one continues to perform

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:52.833
actually without missing a beat.

00:09:54.390 --> 00:09:55.223
I said earlier on,

00:09:55.223 --> 00:09:57.360
I would just comment on
the Social Attitude Survey.

00:09:57.360 --> 00:10:01.350
We've all seen that public
opinion is really clear.

00:10:01.350 --> 00:10:05.160
I mean, clearly, it's helpful
that there's huge support

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:08.190
for the foundational
principles of the NHS,

00:10:08.190 --> 00:10:12.630
but we have to also recognise
actually that the public,

00:10:12.630 --> 00:10:15.120
there's a further decline
in the public confidence

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:16.707
in the performance of the NHS

00:10:16.707 --> 00:10:18.423
and we need to reflect upon that.

00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:22.920
I'm not at all going to be pollyannaish.

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:26.340
I clearly, recognise
that public perception,

00:10:26.340 --> 00:10:28.030
but I would like to say that

00:10:29.730 --> 00:10:32.460
the NHS today is providing record levels

00:10:32.460 --> 00:10:34.620
of healthcare intervention.

00:10:34.620 --> 00:10:38.550
And that's been provided by a workforce

00:10:38.550 --> 00:10:42.090
under very real pressure
and working hugely hard

00:10:42.090 --> 00:10:44.040
actually to provide those record levels

00:10:44.040 --> 00:10:46.770
of healthcare intervention.

00:10:46.770 --> 00:10:50.460
And whether it's the fact
that more than 1 in 10

00:10:50.460 --> 00:10:52.770
of the population in England

00:10:52.770 --> 00:10:56.310
has a primary care
appointment every single week,

00:10:56.310 --> 00:10:58.080
I realise access is a major issue.

00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:01.050
We've got to do better
at how we provide access.

00:11:01.050 --> 00:11:04.590
But in primary care, more
than 1 in 10 of the population

00:11:04.590 --> 00:11:06.630
has an appointment every single week.

00:11:06.630 --> 00:11:09.300
And almost half of those, it's about 48%,

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:10.650
but don't quote me on that number,

00:11:10.650 --> 00:11:12.510
but is I think it is the right number.

00:11:12.510 --> 00:11:14.640
So almost half of those appointments

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:16.890
were on the day that
the individual patient

00:11:16.890 --> 00:11:20.130
or citizen asks for the appointment.

00:11:20.130 --> 00:11:22.110
And it's about 70% of these appointments

00:11:22.110 --> 00:11:23.591
are physical appointments,

00:11:23.591 --> 00:11:26.370
and around a third or just under a third

00:11:26.370 --> 00:11:29.760
are virtual or telephony appointments,

00:11:29.760 --> 00:11:33.180
callbacks on tests,
blood tests, et cetera.

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:36.090
But I think we shouldn't
lose sight, actually,

00:11:36.090 --> 00:11:38.030
just with the sheer scale of provision.

00:11:38.030 --> 00:11:40.853
At the same time, we have
to recognise that the public

00:11:40.853 --> 00:11:45.853
has in that survey expressed
concern around performance.

00:11:46.410 --> 00:11:50.070
The other thing I often like to share,

00:11:50.070 --> 00:11:52.380
which I learn actually from all of you,

00:11:52.380 --> 00:11:54.900
but also in particular from Amanda,

00:11:54.900 --> 00:11:57.333
is actually it's not just a
scale of the intervention,

00:11:57.333 --> 00:12:01.290
it's the level of innovation that we have

00:12:01.290 --> 00:12:04.830
and we demonstrate all
the time in the NHS.

00:12:04.830 --> 00:12:09.420
And I did an event actually
at Deloitte's Academy

00:12:09.420 --> 00:12:11.337
two mornings ago on productivity.

00:12:11.337 --> 00:12:13.410
And I did that with May Li,

00:12:13.410 --> 00:12:15.750
who's our Director of Efficiency.

00:12:15.750 --> 00:12:16.977
And again, one of the things that came up

00:12:16.977 --> 00:12:19.410
was the topic of innovation

00:12:19.410 --> 00:12:23.340
and the ability to talk
about our genomics activity

00:12:23.340 --> 00:12:27.480
to talk about that rather
amazing announcement of last week

00:12:27.480 --> 00:12:29.910
on ctDNA for lung cancer,

00:12:29.910 --> 00:12:31.560
which actually removes the need

00:12:31.560 --> 00:12:33.210
or reduces the need for endoscopy

00:12:33.210 --> 00:12:35.842
and allows blood tests to identify,

00:12:35.842 --> 00:12:37.410
identify that cancer,

00:12:37.410 --> 00:12:39.030
then to intervene much more quickly,

00:12:39.030 --> 00:12:41.970
whether it's cytosponges,
whether in technology,

00:12:41.970 --> 00:12:46.970
it's the NHS app, which
continues with huge support

00:12:47.430 --> 00:12:48.900
from the Department of Health

00:12:48.900 --> 00:12:51.240
actually to continues to improve

00:12:51.240 --> 00:12:53.247
and to add functionality all the time

00:12:53.247 --> 00:12:57.300
and to see significant take
up from the population.

00:12:57.300 --> 00:12:58.530
And all of us, I think would agree

00:12:58.530 --> 00:13:00.870
that if you look at the NHS app,

00:13:00.870 --> 00:13:05.430
it is going to continue to
transform, if not revolutionise,

00:13:05.430 --> 00:13:08.670
the way that we offer healthcare,

00:13:08.670 --> 00:13:12.990
health intervention to the public.

00:13:12.990 --> 00:13:15.150
But the Social Attitudes Survey

00:13:15.150 --> 00:13:17.910
makes for very sobering reading

00:13:17.910 --> 00:13:19.590
and we have to clearly reflect on that,

00:13:19.590 --> 00:13:22.350
but then continue with our workforce

00:13:22.350 --> 00:13:26.160
to continue to provide this level of care.

00:13:26.160 --> 00:13:28.380
I'd just like to talk
very briefly if I might,

00:13:28.380 --> 00:13:30.600
on industrial action.

00:13:30.600 --> 00:13:33.090
And I would say, let me
link that first of all,

00:13:33.090 --> 00:13:36.243
to elective backlog and waiting lists.

00:13:37.748 --> 00:13:40.500
Through the work of the NHS staff,

00:13:40.500 --> 00:13:42.540
there is continued progress,

00:13:42.540 --> 00:13:45.990
good progress on the
longest waits for patients.

00:13:45.990 --> 00:13:49.530
And this month's data showed
that the COVID backlog fell

00:13:49.530 --> 00:13:54.270
for the fourth consecutive month.

00:13:54.270 --> 00:13:56.970
But when you think about the
impact of industrial action

00:13:56.970 --> 00:13:58.983
and also a busy winter period,

00:14:00.004 --> 00:14:02.010
I think the progress has been good that

00:14:02.010 --> 00:14:02.850
for four months running,

00:14:02.850 --> 00:14:05.670
we've seen a fall in that waiting list.

00:14:05.670 --> 00:14:10.670
And that is thanks to the hugely
hard work of the front line

00:14:11.010 --> 00:14:13.560
and also the planning
and the engagement with

00:14:13.560 --> 00:14:16.470
topic by a number of our leaders.

00:14:16.470 --> 00:14:18.450
However, the BMA did announce this month

00:14:18.450 --> 00:14:21.030
that they're going to extend their mandate

00:14:21.030 --> 00:14:22.680
for further industrial action,

00:14:22.680 --> 00:14:24.990
which obviously is a concern for all of us

00:14:24.990 --> 00:14:28.020
and it does make it harder to continue

00:14:28.020 --> 00:14:31.353
positive trajectory on the electives.

00:14:32.940 --> 00:14:37.940
An estimated 430,000 fewer
treatments were delivered

00:14:38.550 --> 00:14:42.907
due to the industrial action
since it began in December '22.

00:14:44.130 --> 00:14:45.360
And actually the industrial action

00:14:45.360 --> 00:14:47.550
has had a particular
impact on our ambition

00:14:47.550 --> 00:14:49.980
to bring down the longest waits.

00:14:49.980 --> 00:14:54.980
So 157,000 fewer treatments
by the end of March

00:14:56.517 --> 00:15:00.000
for patients to avoid waiting
for more than 65 weeks.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:04.530
So their treatments were delayed 157,000,

00:15:04.530 --> 00:15:06.480
you might otherwise, I think,

00:15:06.480 --> 00:15:08.730
have actually then been treated in that

00:15:08.730 --> 00:15:12.000
and that particular
cohort in the electives

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:14.220
or in the waiting lists
would've then been reduced.

00:15:14.220 --> 00:15:17.160
So we're seeing real delay actually

00:15:17.160 --> 00:15:21.873
because of industrial action
in the treatment of patients.

00:15:22.910 --> 00:15:24.650
I just say planning guidance.

00:15:24.650 --> 00:15:27.600
So I think Amanda's probably
going to comment more

00:15:27.600 --> 00:15:28.590
on the planning guidance.

00:15:28.590 --> 00:15:31.350
It was published I think late yesterday,

00:15:31.350 --> 00:15:34.140
and it sets out the
priorities for next year

00:15:34.140 --> 00:15:37.740
and I know that we will be
continuing to discuss those

00:15:37.740 --> 00:15:40.170
not while we discussed them this morning.

00:15:40.170 --> 00:15:41.850
And we'll continue to discuss those

00:15:41.850 --> 00:15:43.020
a little bit through this meeting,

00:15:43.020 --> 00:15:45.720
but also in future meetings.

00:15:45.720 --> 00:15:47.910
So that's all I think I'm going to say

00:15:47.910 --> 00:15:50.670
by way of introduction.

00:15:50.670 --> 00:15:55.567
Apart from, we intend
to announce next week,

00:15:55.567 --> 00:15:57.083
something we'd flagged earlier when

00:15:57.083 --> 00:15:59.408
a number of our new colleagues

00:15:59.408 --> 00:16:02.070
who have joined us on
the board were announced.

00:16:02.070 --> 00:16:04.830
So we've had four new
directors have joined us,

00:16:04.830 --> 00:16:07.440
but actually, we intend
to announce two associate

00:16:07.440 --> 00:16:10.620
non-executive directors in
the course of next week.

00:16:10.620 --> 00:16:14.340
One of whom very able in
the world of workforce

00:16:14.340 --> 00:16:18.270
and talent and training and
development and the other.

00:16:18.270 --> 00:16:21.750
Again, very expert in
the world of actually

00:16:21.750 --> 00:16:26.750
large retail technology systems,
legacy systems and cloud.

00:16:27.270 --> 00:16:29.880
So I'll be delighted when we
can make that announcement

00:16:29.880 --> 00:16:33.750
and they will then join us
I think for the next board.

00:16:33.750 --> 00:16:35.280
Amanda, over to you.

00:16:35.280 --> 00:16:37.377
- Richard, thank you very much indeed.

00:16:37.377 --> 00:16:41.820
So I will attempt not to
duplicate what Richard said,

00:16:41.820 --> 00:16:43.470
but we will just touch on
some of the same things

00:16:43.470 --> 00:16:44.303
'cause I want to build on some of-

00:16:44.303 --> 00:16:45.516
- Sorry.
- No, no, no, no.

00:16:45.516 --> 00:16:47.670
Completely right.

00:16:47.670 --> 00:16:49.320
But just to go a little bit deeper,

00:16:49.320 --> 00:16:52.920
I think particularly on planning
guidance, if that's okay.

00:16:52.920 --> 00:16:57.030
but just to say, of course,
since we were last together

00:16:57.030 --> 00:17:02.030
as a board, we have heard the
Chancellor's Spring Budget

00:17:03.390 --> 00:17:08.390
and that contained some
quite big news for the NHS.

00:17:08.910 --> 00:17:10.620
So in the current climate,

00:17:10.620 --> 00:17:14.010
an extra 2.45 billion of funding

00:17:14.010 --> 00:17:16.560
for next year is very welcome.

00:17:16.560 --> 00:17:19.890
It does allow us to go into '24, '25

00:17:19.890 --> 00:17:23.310
with a flat reel financial position.

00:17:23.310 --> 00:17:27.120
So whilst it will still be

00:17:27.120 --> 00:17:30.660
a very financially challenging year,

00:17:30.660 --> 00:17:33.090
I think it means that we
clearly need to continue

00:17:33.090 --> 00:17:36.930
to make sure that we are
spending every penny wisely

00:17:36.930 --> 00:17:40.560
ringing out the maximum
benefit for patients.

00:17:40.560 --> 00:17:41.970
And it also means though,

00:17:41.970 --> 00:17:45.900
that we can keep making
progress on the priorities

00:17:45.900 --> 00:17:47.310
that matter most for patients.

00:17:47.310 --> 00:17:48.240
And I'll say a bit more about that

00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:50.940
in relation to planning
guidance in a moment.

00:17:50.940 --> 00:17:53.700
The other big announcement
for us in the spring budget

00:17:53.700 --> 00:17:56.130
was the very significant
capital investment.

00:17:56.130 --> 00:17:57.210
You already mentioned it, Richard,

00:17:57.210 --> 00:17:59.730
of 3.4 billion in technology

00:17:59.730 --> 00:18:02.730
and data starting from '25, '26.

00:18:02.730 --> 00:18:04.560
That is really positive news.

00:18:04.560 --> 00:18:07.017
So it will allow us to roll out our tech

00:18:07.017 --> 00:18:10.470
and digital services to
improve access waiting times

00:18:10.470 --> 00:18:12.930
and outcomes and has the potential,

00:18:12.930 --> 00:18:14.190
I would honestly say this,

00:18:14.190 --> 00:18:16.650
to be genuinely transformational

00:18:16.650 --> 00:18:19.590
for patients and for our staff.

00:18:19.590 --> 00:18:20.730
So we'll hear shortly,

00:18:20.730 --> 00:18:22.530
I think from Julian a little bit more

00:18:22.530 --> 00:18:24.090
on the wider implications of the budget.

00:18:24.090 --> 00:18:26.370
And I think in due
course we will come back,

00:18:26.370 --> 00:18:28.350
I'm sure many times to talking about

00:18:28.350 --> 00:18:31.923
the bigger tech digital
data opportunities.

00:18:32.790 --> 00:18:35.220
On planning guidance, so
as Richard has just said,

00:18:35.220 --> 00:18:38.670
colleagues will know that that
was issued yesterday evening.

00:18:38.670 --> 00:18:39.933
So I think we would obviously acknowledge

00:18:39.933 --> 00:18:43.080
that that was somewhat later than usual.

00:18:43.080 --> 00:18:47.340
Some of that was though
because a lot of the things

00:18:47.340 --> 00:18:49.770
I've just mentioned in the
budget were very material

00:18:49.770 --> 00:18:51.390
to being able to land the detail

00:18:51.390 --> 00:18:54.240
that was then set out in
planning guidance yesterday.

00:18:54.240 --> 00:18:56.700
What hopefully it does mean for colleagues

00:18:56.700 --> 00:18:59.280
around the room plus
anybody who is listening in

00:18:59.280 --> 00:19:01.140
is there aren't many, if any,

00:19:01.140 --> 00:19:04.170
surprises in the planning guidance.

00:19:04.170 --> 00:19:07.590
However, saying that it
is a vital piece of work

00:19:07.590 --> 00:19:09.180
and it's a really important document

00:19:09.180 --> 00:19:11.910
because it does offer a clear articulation

00:19:11.910 --> 00:19:13.560
of our collective ambition

00:19:13.560 --> 00:19:15.570
and the ambition we have
agreed with government

00:19:15.570 --> 00:19:17.070
over the next 12 months.

00:19:17.070 --> 00:19:22.070
And it also shows a clear, a
continued direction of travel.

00:19:22.470 --> 00:19:24.420
It also because it's an annual thing,

00:19:24.420 --> 00:19:26.850
gives us a chance to look back at the year

00:19:26.850 --> 00:19:29.400
that we have just concluded

00:19:29.400 --> 00:19:31.050
or we're just about to conclude.

00:19:31.050 --> 00:19:33.157
And I think as Richard has said,

00:19:33.157 --> 00:19:34.620
"It does allow us to highlight

00:19:34.620 --> 00:19:36.990
some of the things that sort
of despite the challenges

00:19:36.990 --> 00:19:40.140
we can be collectively really proud of."

00:19:40.140 --> 00:19:41.580
So over the past year,

00:19:41.580 --> 00:19:43.590
NHS teams have made significant progress

00:19:43.590 --> 00:19:47.517
delivering key priorities
for patients in the face of,

00:19:47.517 --> 00:19:49.200
and we've just touched on it,

00:19:49.200 --> 00:19:52.320
but strong headwinds
from industrial action,

00:19:52.320 --> 00:19:55.350
increased demand, the ongoing
effects of the pandemic,

00:19:55.350 --> 00:19:58.050
and inflationary pressures.

00:19:58.050 --> 00:20:01.050
We've seen, and Emily's
talked about this before,

00:20:01.050 --> 00:20:05.040
but we've seen real
progress against almost

00:20:05.040 --> 00:20:08.040
every headline objective from last year.

00:20:08.040 --> 00:20:10.500
Whether that's urgent
emergency care, primary care,

00:20:10.500 --> 00:20:13.740
mental health, inequalities,
elective and cancer care,

00:20:13.740 --> 00:20:16.290
or technology and innovation.

00:20:16.290 --> 00:20:18.530
And what's really clear as you look back

00:20:18.530 --> 00:20:20.850
at the year that's just finished is

00:20:20.850 --> 00:20:24.269
all of that has taken careful planning,

00:20:24.269 --> 00:20:27.660
hard work, and enormous ingenuity.

00:20:27.660 --> 00:20:32.400
And that is all thanks to the
commitment, the adaptability,

00:20:32.400 --> 00:20:36.750
and the professionalism of
staff all across the NHS.

00:20:36.750 --> 00:20:39.240
So that I think is,

00:20:39.240 --> 00:20:41.880
I'll come back to say a bit
more about that in a moment.

00:20:41.880 --> 00:20:45.120
But that is worthy, I
think of note set against

00:20:45.120 --> 00:20:47.430
the fact that of course what
planning guidance sets out

00:20:47.430 --> 00:20:51.150
is the next steps in progress
on all of those things

00:20:51.150 --> 00:20:54.330
and where we need to go next
to continue that journey.

00:20:54.330 --> 00:20:57.690
The other thing that planning
guidance sets out is,

00:20:57.690 --> 00:21:00.150
again, I think building on
what I've just said about

00:21:00.150 --> 00:21:03.720
the spring budget, but the fact
that we are putting in place

00:21:03.720 --> 00:21:05.700
some of the building
blocks for the future.

00:21:05.700 --> 00:21:07.710
So that investment in technology,

00:21:07.710 --> 00:21:10.290
the NHS long-term workforce plan

00:21:10.290 --> 00:21:11.612
that was published last summer

00:21:11.612 --> 00:21:14.670
and actually the increasingly
mature partnerships

00:21:14.670 --> 00:21:18.690
at ICP, ICB, and place level.

00:21:18.690 --> 00:21:21.690
But we do know that
that is not the totality

00:21:21.690 --> 00:21:24.600
of what is needed to ensure
the NHS is better able

00:21:24.600 --> 00:21:27.990
to meet our patient's needs in the future.

00:21:27.990 --> 00:21:29.040
We've talked about it before,

00:21:29.040 --> 00:21:30.750
but with an ageing population

00:21:30.750 --> 00:21:33.030
and more patients with complex needs,

00:21:33.030 --> 00:21:37.500
the NHS and our partners
are going to need to adapt.

00:21:37.500 --> 00:21:38.610
Richard, you've just said it,

00:21:38.610 --> 00:21:41.880
if we're going to help people
live longer, healthier lives.

00:21:41.880 --> 00:21:44.580
So as set out in the
long term workforce plan,

00:21:44.580 --> 00:21:47.100
what that will mean is further investment

00:21:47.100 --> 00:21:49.470
in estates and in social care.

00:21:49.470 --> 00:21:51.750
And crucially, we will also need

00:21:51.750 --> 00:21:56.700
a coordinated ambitious
approach to prevention.

00:21:56.700 --> 00:21:59.760
But we do have a base on which to plan

00:21:59.760 --> 00:22:01.830
service improvement over the medium term.

00:22:01.830 --> 00:22:03.180
And I know I speak for all my colleagues

00:22:03.180 --> 00:22:05.100
when I say we are now really
looking forward to working

00:22:05.100 --> 00:22:08.785
with colleagues in the NHS on
that over the coming months.

00:22:08.785 --> 00:22:10.290
This year's planning guidance.

00:22:10.290 --> 00:22:12.630
Last couple of things on
this before I move on.

00:22:12.630 --> 00:22:15.180
Does also set out the things that we know

00:22:15.180 --> 00:22:17.280
can make a big difference
to the working lives

00:22:17.280 --> 00:22:19.830
of our colleagues, including things like

00:22:19.830 --> 00:22:22.350
increasing choice and flexibility in rotas

00:22:22.350 --> 00:22:25.560
and stronger action on sexual safety.

00:22:25.560 --> 00:22:28.290
Again, both things we've
talked about before.

00:22:28.290 --> 00:22:30.150
But as we say in planning guidance,

00:22:30.150 --> 00:22:33.420
those things should be seen as the floor

00:22:33.420 --> 00:22:36.150
rather than the ceiling
of our collective ambition

00:22:36.150 --> 00:22:39.093
to be a better and more
responsive employer.

00:22:40.560 --> 00:22:41.640
So enough on planning guidance,

00:22:41.640 --> 00:22:42.865
I know we're going to say more about

00:22:42.865 --> 00:22:44.281
it as the meeting goes on.

00:22:44.281 --> 00:22:45.360
Richard, you've talked about

00:22:45.360 --> 00:22:47.343
the British Social Attitudes Survey.

00:22:47.343 --> 00:22:48.176
- Yeah.
- And I think

00:22:48.176 --> 00:22:49.560
I just wanted to make sure

00:22:49.560 --> 00:22:51.960
that I sort of added my
voice to yours on this

00:22:51.960 --> 00:22:55.320
because clearly, the findings
do make difficult reading

00:22:55.320 --> 00:22:56.850
and they do reflect the sustained

00:22:56.850 --> 00:22:58.230
pressure facing our services,

00:22:58.230 --> 00:23:02.070
particularly when the survey
took place back in the autumn.

00:23:02.070 --> 00:23:03.300
But they're also a reminder

00:23:03.300 --> 00:23:06.840
that we are very much still
recovering from the pandemic

00:23:06.840 --> 00:23:09.870
whilst also dealing with
all the knock on impacts

00:23:09.870 --> 00:23:10.977
of industrial action,

00:23:10.977 --> 00:23:13.620
and the other headwinds I've talked about.

00:23:13.620 --> 00:23:16.620
And whilst there is always welcome to see

00:23:16.620 --> 00:23:18.570
that the public overwhelmingly continue

00:23:18.570 --> 00:23:21.630
to back the founding
principles of the NHS,

00:23:21.630 --> 00:23:24.058
and it is also true
that our recovery plans

00:23:24.058 --> 00:23:26.910
are improving access and helping the NHS

00:23:26.910 --> 00:23:29.910
to deliver year on year
improvements for patients.

00:23:29.910 --> 00:23:33.900
This must act as just a
really important insight

00:23:33.900 --> 00:23:36.390
for everyone working in the NHS on how

00:23:36.390 --> 00:23:40.200
the public currently
feel about our services,

00:23:40.200 --> 00:23:44.190
and it is a timely reminder of
how much hard work is ahead.

00:23:44.190 --> 00:23:45.930
And while we continue to make progress

00:23:45.930 --> 00:23:48.120
on all of our recovery plans,

00:23:48.120 --> 00:23:51.183
we know there is still a long way to go.

00:23:53.460 --> 00:23:54.293
I'm going to move on
- Yeah.

00:23:54.293 --> 00:23:56.073
- A few other things to talk about.

00:23:57.829 --> 00:24:02.790
Because I know we've talked
about this in various forms,

00:24:02.790 --> 00:24:06.630
but not as a board, I did want
to just take this opportunity

00:24:06.630 --> 00:24:10.200
to say that I know again,
I speak for many of us,

00:24:10.200 --> 00:24:11.820
all of us I'm sure to say,

00:24:11.820 --> 00:24:15.030
how sorry I was to hear
the Princess of Wales' news

00:24:15.030 --> 00:24:17.223
about her cancer diagnosis.

00:24:18.077 --> 00:24:20.430
And I think it's probably a reminder

00:24:20.430 --> 00:24:22.140
that receiving a cancer diagnosis

00:24:22.140 --> 00:24:25.530
at any time can be hugely daunting.

00:24:25.530 --> 00:24:28.470
But by bravely speaking out in the way

00:24:28.470 --> 00:24:32.340
that the Princess of Wales
has done about her diagnosis,

00:24:32.340 --> 00:24:34.050
we know that that will, in fact,

00:24:34.050 --> 00:24:37.140
it already has encouraged
others to do the same

00:24:37.140 --> 00:24:40.170
and by the simple act of raising awareness

00:24:40.170 --> 00:24:42.510
that will save lives.

00:24:42.510 --> 00:24:46.530
So we've seen cancer information
pages on the NHS website.

00:24:46.530 --> 00:24:49.440
I've had a huge spike in visits

00:24:49.440 --> 00:24:51.570
since the Princess' video message.

00:24:51.570 --> 00:24:53.130
I can tell you, just checking the stats,

00:24:53.130 --> 00:24:56.790
there were 2,840 visits
to the page on cancer

00:24:56.790 --> 00:24:59.250
in the 24 hours after the announcement.

00:24:59.250 --> 00:25:04.250
That is a rise of 373% just
over that 24 hour period.

00:25:05.790 --> 00:25:08.973
So we know, we talk about
it a lot at this board

00:25:08.973 --> 00:25:13.710
that the earlier that you can
detect cancer, the better,

00:25:13.710 --> 00:25:15.570
because the earlier that can be diagnosed

00:25:15.570 --> 00:25:19.620
means you have a higher chance
of successful treatment.

00:25:19.620 --> 00:25:22.170
And so whilst we are diagnosing

00:25:22.170 --> 00:25:26.820
early stage cancer at record,

00:25:26.820 --> 00:25:29.250
well, we're diagnosing
more cancers together,

00:25:29.250 --> 00:25:32.070
really precise at an
earlier stage than ever.

00:25:32.070 --> 00:25:35.040
And survival rates are
at an all time high.

00:25:35.040 --> 00:25:39.630
We do want to continue to
encourage people to come forward

00:25:39.630 --> 00:25:43.320
when they are concerned
about symptoms, not wait.

00:25:43.320 --> 00:25:45.900
So anyone with unusual worrying symptoms

00:25:45.900 --> 00:25:47.250
should visit their GP,

00:25:47.250 --> 00:25:50.460
and it's just again a reminder
of how important it is

00:25:50.460 --> 00:25:52.953
to take up screening
appointments when offered.

00:25:53.850 --> 00:25:55.320
Richard, I'm going to finish if I may,

00:25:55.320 --> 00:25:58.320
with just a bit more of a look back.

00:25:58.320 --> 00:26:00.648
So it's one of the things that we,

00:26:00.648 --> 00:26:01.481
I've just talked about

00:26:01.481 --> 00:26:03.390
that we did as part of planning guidance.

00:26:03.390 --> 00:26:06.000
And you're right to say, Richard,

00:26:06.000 --> 00:26:08.970
and you always remind us of
this, not to be pollyannaish,

00:26:08.970 --> 00:26:11.610
not to sound like we haven't noticed

00:26:11.610 --> 00:26:13.500
the level of pressure on our staff

00:26:13.500 --> 00:26:15.180
or indeed some of the challenges

00:26:15.180 --> 00:26:16.950
that our patients are facing.

00:26:16.950 --> 00:26:20.820
However, I did a visit last,
a couple of weeks ago now,

00:26:20.820 --> 00:26:23.700
maybe three weeks ago,
which was just, again,

00:26:23.700 --> 00:26:26.910
a really timely reminder of
despite some of those pressures,

00:26:26.910 --> 00:26:29.250
how much progress is being made.

00:26:29.250 --> 00:26:32.714
So I visited the National
Problem Gambling Clinic in London

00:26:32.714 --> 00:26:34.320
now five years ago,

00:26:34.320 --> 00:26:37.830
that was the only clinic
providing specialised treatment

00:26:37.830 --> 00:26:39.720
for people with gambling addictions.

00:26:39.720 --> 00:26:43.890
Now we've just opened our
15th clinic in Sheffield

00:26:43.890 --> 00:26:47.100
and that meets the NHS
long-term plan target.

00:26:47.100 --> 00:26:49.048
But it's just one of those things

00:26:49.048 --> 00:26:51.540
that might not always get the front pages,

00:26:51.540 --> 00:26:53.940
but actually, it's
really important progress

00:26:53.940 --> 00:26:56.250
for those patients.

00:26:56.250 --> 00:27:01.250
And it's, I think again,
just a moment to say,

00:27:01.687 --> 00:27:04.080
"We said we would do
something, we've done it."

00:27:04.080 --> 00:27:06.623
We already know what a big
difference that's making.

00:27:06.623 --> 00:27:10.320
So if I just look back at the
previous year for a moment.

00:27:10.320 --> 00:27:12.720
So we said we would open

00:27:12.720 --> 00:27:16.740
5,000 additional core beds, G&amp;A beds,

00:27:16.740 --> 00:27:19.770
we said we'd have them
open in January and we did.

00:27:19.770 --> 00:27:23.580
And that along with more
ambulance hours on the road,

00:27:23.580 --> 00:27:25.680
greater use of urgent community response

00:27:25.680 --> 00:27:27.240
and admission avoidance services,

00:27:27.240 --> 00:27:29.524
increasingly mature discharge hubs

00:27:29.524 --> 00:27:33.000
and over 11,000 virtual ward beds

00:27:33.000 --> 00:27:37.320
has made a real difference to
urgent emergency care flow.

00:27:37.320 --> 00:27:38.790
I've said it in planning guidance,

00:27:38.790 --> 00:27:42.300
but it is worth noting that
for the first time since 2009,

00:27:42.300 --> 00:27:45.030
apart from that very first
year of the COVID pandemic

00:27:45.030 --> 00:27:49.290
four hour A&amp;E performance has improved.

00:27:49.290 --> 00:27:50.550
And actually over the years,

00:27:50.550 --> 00:27:53.880
so it's an average category
two ambulance response times

00:27:53.880 --> 00:27:56.670
reduced this year by 17 minutes.

00:27:56.670 --> 00:27:58.349
In mental health, we've expanded services

00:27:58.349 --> 00:28:00.840
with four in 10 schools now having access

00:28:00.840 --> 00:28:02.700
to mental health support teams

00:28:02.700 --> 00:28:04.620
and more people than ever before

00:28:04.620 --> 00:28:07.020
in contact with NHS services for support

00:28:07.020 --> 00:28:09.300
for their mental health, autism,

00:28:09.300 --> 00:28:11.430
and or learning disabilities.

00:28:11.430 --> 00:28:15.090
We've had record numbers
of people coming forward

00:28:15.090 --> 00:28:18.180
and being checked and treated for cancer.

00:28:18.180 --> 00:28:19.110
And thanks to initiatives

00:28:19.110 --> 00:28:21.120
like our targeted Lung Health Checks,

00:28:21.120 --> 00:28:23.340
we're now diagnosing,
as I said a moment ago,

00:28:23.340 --> 00:28:25.650
more cancers at stage one and stage two

00:28:25.650 --> 00:28:27.993
when cancer is easier to treat.

00:28:28.830 --> 00:28:30.000
You've mentioned industrial action

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:32.790
and the impact it's had
on elective care, Richard,

00:28:32.790 --> 00:28:37.350
but despite that long waits
for elective care have reduced

00:28:37.350 --> 00:28:40.118
and we've done over the
course of this year,

00:28:40.118 --> 00:28:45.118
17.3 million elective
pathways have been completed.

00:28:45.600 --> 00:28:47.670
That is in 2023,

00:28:47.670 --> 00:28:51.297
that's an increase of over
1.3 million treatments

00:28:51.297 --> 00:28:52.947
compared to the year before.

00:28:52.947 --> 00:28:56.130
And that is despite the
disruption of industrial action,

00:28:56.130 --> 00:28:58.923
which would've allowed us to do even more.

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:03.210
We've seen nearly 20
million more appointments

00:29:03.210 --> 00:29:04.707
in primary care than last year.

00:29:04.707 --> 00:29:07.590
And that means as Rich as often says,

00:29:07.590 --> 00:29:10.020
you've given your 1 in
10 of the population,

00:29:10.020 --> 00:29:14.400
I'm going to do my 1.4 million
people every working day

00:29:14.400 --> 00:29:16.620
with an appointment in primary care.

00:29:16.620 --> 00:29:20.490
We've also launched new
initiatives like Pharmacy First

00:29:20.490 --> 00:29:22.770
and we've also published
the Dental Recovery Plan,

00:29:22.770 --> 00:29:24.690
all of which are designed
to make it easier

00:29:24.690 --> 00:29:27.420
for patients to access
care when they need it.

00:29:27.420 --> 00:29:28.920
And alongside all of that,

00:29:28.920 --> 00:29:31.920
we've continued to
develop and to adopt new

00:29:31.920 --> 00:29:33.060
and innovative treatments,

00:29:33.060 --> 00:29:36.000
whether that's Liquid
Biopsy Testing for patients

00:29:36.000 --> 00:29:37.680
with suspected lung cancer

00:29:37.680 --> 00:29:40.890
or it's the rollout of the
latest diabetes technology

00:29:40.890 --> 00:29:42.810
to provide tens of thousands of people

00:29:42.810 --> 00:29:45.858
with life-changing devices
to manage their condition.

00:29:45.858 --> 00:29:50.858
So just finish by saying,
we are not alone as an NHS,

00:29:51.480 --> 00:29:54.480
in fact, it is completely in common

00:29:54.480 --> 00:29:58.080
with advanced healthcare
systems across the world.

00:29:58.080 --> 00:30:01.200
We are all of us facing major challenges

00:30:01.200 --> 00:30:02.910
in recovering services

00:30:02.910 --> 00:30:06.630
and meeting the growing needs
of an ageing population.

00:30:06.630 --> 00:30:11.190
But this year the NHS has once again shown

00:30:11.190 --> 00:30:13.530
that it can rise to the challenge

00:30:13.530 --> 00:30:16.710
and real improvement is possible

00:30:16.710 --> 00:30:19.920
even in the toughest of circumstances.

00:30:19.920 --> 00:30:22.740
So I'd like to finish my remarks

00:30:22.740 --> 00:30:25.710
by just saying an enormous
thank you to staff

00:30:25.710 --> 00:30:28.590
across the NHS for
everything you have done

00:30:28.590 --> 00:30:32.100
in the past 12 months for our patients.

00:30:32.100 --> 00:30:32.933
- Thank you.

00:30:34.396 --> 00:30:36.090
I'm not going to pause for the moment

00:30:36.090 --> 00:30:40.080
for sort of broad comments
on anything either

00:30:40.080 --> 00:30:41.820
that I've said or Amanda has said.

00:30:41.820 --> 00:30:43.170
What I'd like to do is just carry on

00:30:43.170 --> 00:30:45.720
that narrative actually
around performance.

00:30:45.720 --> 00:30:48.441
And Emily, if I can ask
you to give us an update

00:30:48.441 --> 00:30:51.030
on operational performance
and then Julian,

00:30:51.030 --> 00:30:54.060
if that's right, I'll come to
you for financial performance.

00:30:54.060 --> 00:30:55.110
Emily.

00:30:55.110 --> 00:30:56.610
- Thank you.

00:30:56.610 --> 00:31:01.110
So there are two papers
that show the performance

00:31:01.110 --> 00:31:04.200
until the most recent published
data at the end of January,

00:31:04.200 --> 00:31:05.800
some data published in February.

00:31:06.780 --> 00:31:09.600
And I'm going to try and pick
out a few aspects of that

00:31:09.600 --> 00:31:11.523
to draw people's attention to.

00:31:13.590 --> 00:31:17.051
In terms of the pollyanna challenge,

00:31:17.051 --> 00:31:21.120
I like to think of it as an
and in that I hope what I say

00:31:21.120 --> 00:31:23.220
and what the paper presents represents

00:31:23.220 --> 00:31:27.390
really extraordinary efforts
by NHS staff across the board

00:31:27.390 --> 00:31:30.960
and in several places much further to go.

00:31:30.960 --> 00:31:33.693
And I want to make sure we recognise both.

00:31:34.740 --> 00:31:36.840
I think the data provides clear evidence

00:31:36.840 --> 00:31:39.150
of some of the challenges
we've been facing

00:31:39.150 --> 00:31:40.830
and we've been able to face them

00:31:40.830 --> 00:31:42.660
because of that professionalism

00:31:42.660 --> 00:31:44.460
that all NHS staff have shown,

00:31:44.460 --> 00:31:45.570
particularly across winter

00:31:45.570 --> 00:31:47.790
and across periods of industrial action

00:31:47.790 --> 00:31:48.960
to ensure we're providing

00:31:48.960 --> 00:31:51.630
the best possible care for patients.

00:31:51.630 --> 00:31:54.300
So you start with UEC.

00:31:54.300 --> 00:31:56.340
Pressures remained high during February

00:31:56.340 --> 00:31:57.990
with five days of industrial action

00:31:57.990 --> 00:32:01.080
by junior doctors and
high levels of demand

00:32:01.080 --> 00:32:03.990
compared to the previous
year, in particular.

00:32:03.990 --> 00:32:06.450
In February 24, just to give
you a sense of the scale,

00:32:06.450 --> 00:32:08.970
we saw record levels of A&amp;E attendance

00:32:08.970 --> 00:32:11.847
almost 9% higher than February '23.

00:32:11.847 --> 00:32:14.880
And ambulance services
answering 12% more calls

00:32:14.880 --> 00:32:17.700
to 999 than February '23.

00:32:17.700 --> 00:32:20.220
Emergency admissions
have also been increasing

00:32:20.220 --> 00:32:22.047
and have been up 7%, 10%,

00:32:22.047 --> 00:32:24.360
and 12% in each of the last three months

00:32:24.360 --> 00:32:26.520
compared to the previous year.

00:32:26.520 --> 00:32:30.660
We had instead of a
sharp, very high flu peak,

00:32:30.660 --> 00:32:33.360
as in the winter of '22, '23,

00:32:33.360 --> 00:32:36.240
an ongoing flu peak that
was lower at its peak

00:32:36.240 --> 00:32:38.190
but went on for much longer.

00:32:38.190 --> 00:32:41.250
Hospitals had on average
more than 2,000 flu patients

00:32:41.250 --> 00:32:42.660
in hospital beds during February

00:32:42.660 --> 00:32:45.093
compared to 700 in February '23.

00:32:46.050 --> 00:32:48.300
Despite this, the systems have been able

00:32:48.300 --> 00:32:50.610
to deliver four hour
performance improvements

00:32:50.610 --> 00:32:52.580
month on month since December.

00:32:52.580 --> 00:32:57.580
It was at 70.9% in February
compared to 70.3% in January.

00:32:57.960 --> 00:33:00.990
Category two performance
was at 36 minutes 20 seconds

00:33:00.990 --> 00:33:02.150
in February, which is an improvement

00:33:02.150 --> 00:33:04.353
of over three minutes
on the previous month.

00:33:05.190 --> 00:33:07.470
This is in part a reflection
on the significant amount

00:33:07.470 --> 00:33:09.570
of effort and resource that staff invested

00:33:09.570 --> 00:33:12.600
in preparing for and
managing industrial action

00:33:12.600 --> 00:33:14.943
and in recovering from that afterwards.

00:33:16.290 --> 00:33:17.670
A significant amount of work was done

00:33:17.670 --> 00:33:18.660
to prepare for the winter period.

00:33:18.660 --> 00:33:22.830
Amanda has already mentioned
both the core bed commitment

00:33:22.830 --> 00:33:25.530
and the new virtual ward beds.

00:33:25.530 --> 00:33:29.520
So just to put the icing on that cake.

00:33:29.520 --> 00:33:33.150
We delivered in January at the first,

00:33:33.150 --> 00:33:37.139
hitting the target of
the 99,500 core beds.

00:33:37.139 --> 00:33:39.060
And we've maintained that average.

00:33:39.060 --> 00:33:44.060
So the average was 99,765
core beds in place each day.

00:33:44.363 --> 00:33:47.400
And the 10,000 virtual ward bed target

00:33:47.400 --> 00:33:48.510
was delivered ahead of time,

00:33:48.510 --> 00:33:52.263
and in February, that was
11,918 virtual ward beds.

00:33:53.310 --> 00:33:56.970
So what we've been working
on with the UEC team

00:33:56.970 --> 00:33:58.860
and with systems and
with our clinical leaders

00:33:58.860 --> 00:34:02.220
is to figure out how do
we consolidate, maintain,

00:34:02.220 --> 00:34:05.100
and improve that
performance that we've seen,

00:34:05.100 --> 00:34:07.500
the performance improvement
we've seen in UEC.

00:34:07.500 --> 00:34:10.170
We're moving into year two
of the UEC recovery plan.

00:34:10.170 --> 00:34:13.590
We want to sustain the improvements
we made and go further.

00:34:13.590 --> 00:34:16.230
So that includes adjusting areas of focus

00:34:16.230 --> 00:34:18.780
to ensure that patients
get the best possible care

00:34:18.780 --> 00:34:20.700
as we notice what's happening

00:34:20.700 --> 00:34:22.680
in response to the existing targets.

00:34:22.680 --> 00:34:24.840
So for example, I'm sure
people will have noticed

00:34:24.840 --> 00:34:26.580
that in planning guidance
we've asked systems

00:34:26.580 --> 00:34:28.920
to reduce the proportion
of waits over 12 hours

00:34:28.920 --> 00:34:31.650
in A&amp;E compared to '23, '24,

00:34:31.650 --> 00:34:34.383
which is a clinically
critical target for patients.

00:34:35.820 --> 00:34:38.460
If we turn now to elective,

00:34:38.460 --> 00:34:40.140
despite the impact of industrial action,

00:34:40.140 --> 00:34:41.670
which you've both mentioned,

00:34:41.670 --> 00:34:45.000
and just to put a sort
of baseline on that,

00:34:45.000 --> 00:34:46.950
we've seen more than 1 in 10 days

00:34:46.950 --> 00:34:49.560
impacted by strike
action in the past year.

00:34:49.560 --> 00:34:51.090
We are seeing continued progress

00:34:51.090 --> 00:34:52.770
being made to reduce long waits

00:34:52.770 --> 00:34:54.270
with month on month decreases

00:34:54.270 --> 00:34:57.326
seen in the total waiting
list since September '23,

00:34:57.326 --> 00:35:00.543
with a further decrease
seen in January '24.

00:35:01.830 --> 00:35:03.300
I won't read through the numbers

00:35:03.300 --> 00:35:05.580
which are in the pack
in the interest of time,

00:35:05.580 --> 00:35:08.130
but if we just compare that to

00:35:08.130 --> 00:35:10.860
where we were when the elective
plan was first developed

00:35:10.860 --> 00:35:14.040
in January '22, 78 week waits have reduced

00:35:14.040 --> 00:35:16.200
from 78,000 to 14,000,

00:35:16.200 --> 00:35:19.440
and 65 week waits on the way

00:35:19.440 --> 00:35:23.550
to halving from 163,000 to 92,000.

00:35:23.550 --> 00:35:26.160
And overall, our data shows
the total waiting list

00:35:26.160 --> 00:35:28.740
has reduced for four consecutive months

00:35:28.740 --> 00:35:31.020
from August to January '24,

00:35:31.020 --> 00:35:33.510
which is the first time we've
seen consecutive reductions

00:35:33.510 --> 00:35:35.253
since September 2017.

00:35:36.690 --> 00:35:39.390
So none of that happens
without extraordinary levels

00:35:39.390 --> 00:35:41.580
of coordinated work across systems

00:35:41.580 --> 00:35:43.530
and concern at the clinical level

00:35:43.530 --> 00:35:45.243
for individual patient groups.

00:35:46.410 --> 00:35:48.510
What we've learned from all of that work,

00:35:48.510 --> 00:35:50.670
both in UEC in elective and in cancer,

00:35:50.670 --> 00:35:51.990
which I'll come to in a second,

00:35:51.990 --> 00:35:54.360
is that those headline measures

00:35:54.360 --> 00:35:56.490
both in involve good
progress across the board

00:35:56.490 --> 00:35:59.580
and some improvements
that every system has made

00:35:59.580 --> 00:36:00.870
to make things better for patients.

00:36:00.870 --> 00:36:02.340
And in other places where there is

00:36:02.340 --> 00:36:05.520
a specific challenge in
an individual system.

00:36:05.520 --> 00:36:08.370
So for example, the point we're
at now with 78 week waits,

00:36:08.370 --> 00:36:10.560
which is sort of six weeks
post the published data,

00:36:10.560 --> 00:36:12.330
which is in the paper,

00:36:12.330 --> 00:36:14.550
is we can see that the challenges

00:36:14.550 --> 00:36:16.491
are in a specific group of trusts.

00:36:16.491 --> 00:36:18.600
So for example, on 78 week waits,

00:36:18.600 --> 00:36:20.640
the top 13 providers nationally

00:36:20.640 --> 00:36:23.940
account for over 50%
of the national volume.

00:36:23.940 --> 00:36:26.010
And within that there
are specific specialties

00:36:26.010 --> 00:36:28.539
that are challenging
in individual systems.

00:36:28.539 --> 00:36:31.560
So I turn now to cancer at

00:36:31.560 --> 00:36:33.210
just like the other two
areas I've mentioned,

00:36:33.210 --> 00:36:35.910
we're seeing an ongoing
high level of demand.

00:36:35.910 --> 00:36:38.820
So the number of urgent
suspected cancer referrals

00:36:38.820 --> 00:36:42.760
first seen is over 128%
of pre-pandemic levels

00:36:43.890 --> 00:36:46.410
and within that systems
have been able to deliver.

00:36:46.410 --> 00:36:50.250
So performance against the
75% faster diagnostic standard

00:36:50.250 --> 00:36:54.690
was 70.9%, which is four
percentage points higher

00:36:54.690 --> 00:36:57.960
than January '23, which
continues the trend

00:36:57.960 --> 00:37:00.000
to performance being significantly higher

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:01.773
every month, year on year.

00:37:02.670 --> 00:37:04.680
On diagnostics, our
published statistics show

00:37:04.680 --> 00:37:07.170
we're delivering record
diagnostic activity.

00:37:07.170 --> 00:37:10.590
It's 113% above pre-pandemic levels

00:37:10.590 --> 00:37:13.890
and each month in '23,
'24 saw higher activity

00:37:13.890 --> 00:37:16.200
than the same month in any previous year,

00:37:16.200 --> 00:37:20.643
including 2.34 million
diagnostic events in January '24.

00:37:21.840 --> 00:37:24.450
I also wanted to make sure
we spoke about mental health,

00:37:24.450 --> 00:37:26.970
which is an important strand
of our national commitments.

00:37:26.970 --> 00:37:29.790
And where there is also
incredibly high demand.

00:37:29.790 --> 00:37:33.300
There were 116,561 referrals

00:37:33.300 --> 00:37:35.454
to community crisis in January '24,

00:37:35.454 --> 00:37:39.210
which is an 11% increase on January '23.

00:37:39.210 --> 00:37:41.880
The mental health UEC
pathway bed occupancy

00:37:41.880 --> 00:37:44.100
remains very high with
an average length of stay

00:37:44.100 --> 00:37:47.550
at 46 days nationally as of December '23.

00:37:47.550 --> 00:37:49.950
And we still have very
high out of area placements

00:37:49.950 --> 00:37:52.263
because of that high bed occupancy.

00:37:53.430 --> 00:37:55.170
There are places in mental health

00:37:55.170 --> 00:37:56.550
where we can really see the and

00:37:56.550 --> 00:37:58.110
that I mentioned coming together.

00:37:58.110 --> 00:38:01.200
So although in many places
we haven't hit the target

00:38:01.200 --> 00:38:02.940
we were aiming for, we are close,

00:38:02.940 --> 00:38:05.190
and can see it's within reach.

00:38:05.190 --> 00:38:08.370
And we need to support systems
to deliver that this year.

00:38:08.370 --> 00:38:12.510
So for example, the dementia
diagnosis rate is at 64.4%

00:38:12.510 --> 00:38:16.410
as of January '24
against a target of 66.7%

00:38:16.410 --> 00:38:18.960
or an ambition, sorry,
which is demonstrating

00:38:18.960 --> 00:38:21.720
steady improvement since January '23.

00:38:21.720 --> 00:38:23.670
If we can maintain the
existing trajectory,

00:38:23.670 --> 00:38:26.733
we would hope to deliver
the ambition in '24, '25.

00:38:27.990 --> 00:38:30.510
The mental health UEC
pathway bed occupancy

00:38:30.510 --> 00:38:32.133
as I mentioned, remains high,

00:38:33.000 --> 00:38:34.440
and there is more work to do

00:38:34.440 --> 00:38:36.390
to make sure beds are in the right place

00:38:36.390 --> 00:38:37.530
when patients need them,

00:38:37.530 --> 00:38:40.383
including reducing the
46 day length of stay.

00:38:41.640 --> 00:38:43.800
Children and young
people accessing services

00:38:43.800 --> 00:38:48.330
continues to increase
with 758,485 children,

00:38:48.330 --> 00:38:49.950
young people accessing services

00:38:49.950 --> 00:38:52.500
in the 12 months to January '24.

00:38:52.500 --> 00:38:56.700
That's against a target of 840,254.

00:38:56.700 --> 00:38:58.890
So although we're behind trajectory,

00:38:58.890 --> 00:39:01.140
access rates are improving

00:39:01.140 --> 00:39:03.180
and the systems are putting plans in place

00:39:03.180 --> 00:39:05.880
to make sure we can reach that ambition.

00:39:05.880 --> 00:39:08.460
Access to specialist community,
perinatal mental health

00:39:08.460 --> 00:39:09.960
and maternal mental health services

00:39:09.960 --> 00:39:13.679
also continues to increase in
the 12 months to January '24,

00:39:13.679 --> 00:39:17.790
55,873 women access these services

00:39:17.790 --> 00:39:20.943
against the '23, '24 target of 66,000.

00:39:22.500 --> 00:39:25.050
So overall, I hope that conveys the sense

00:39:25.050 --> 00:39:26.940
of extraordinary delivery

00:39:26.940 --> 00:39:29.940
in an ongoing challenging situation

00:39:29.940 --> 00:39:31.530
and a very clear understanding

00:39:31.530 --> 00:39:33.660
that we have more to
do and plans to do so,

00:39:33.660 --> 00:39:35.550
some of which systems will be thinking

00:39:35.550 --> 00:39:36.420
through having received

00:39:36.420 --> 00:39:38.901
