00:44:19.170 --> 00:44:20.850
even earlier in the year to some extent

00:44:20.850 --> 00:44:22.833
than we did last year.
- Very good.

00:44:24.324 --> 00:44:25.650
- To make sure that that systems

00:44:25.650 --> 00:44:28.563
have what they need to manage next winter.

00:44:29.940 --> 00:44:34.440
We can't predict exactly what
the pattern of flu will be,

00:44:34.440 --> 00:44:36.030
but I think we will say,

00:44:36.030 --> 00:44:39.960
the pattern in the winter
of 2023 was one large,

00:44:39.960 --> 00:44:42.210
very large flu spike

00:44:42.210 --> 00:44:44.910
at the same time as increased COVID cases.

00:44:44.910 --> 00:44:49.740
What we saw this time
was a lower flu spike,

00:44:49.740 --> 00:44:51.570
which started a bit later,

00:44:51.570 --> 00:44:53.610
and we thought was coming
down but then actually wasn't.

00:44:53.610 --> 00:44:55.590
It plateaued really effectively

00:44:55.590 --> 00:44:57.100
through January and into February.

00:44:57.100 --> 00:44:58.590
- Yeah.
- And we're still seeing

00:44:58.590 --> 00:45:01.050
more flu patients in beds
than we were last year

00:45:01.050 --> 00:45:02.939
and there was also RSV around,

00:45:02.939 --> 00:45:04.530
which is coming down now too.

00:45:04.530 --> 00:45:06.930
So it's not so much that one win,

00:45:06.930 --> 00:45:09.720
well, very big spikes
obviously very hard to handle,

00:45:09.720 --> 00:45:14.720
but ongoing increased
acute not just presentation

00:45:15.570 --> 00:45:17.434
but acuity of respiratory disease

00:45:17.434 --> 00:45:20.700
has a challenging impact on bed occupancy,

00:45:20.700 --> 00:45:21.600
et cetera, as we're seeing.

00:45:21.600 --> 00:45:24.420
So we are planning for next winter.

00:45:24.420 --> 00:45:26.400
We will keep on learning as we go

00:45:26.400 --> 00:45:28.260
from what systems experience

00:45:28.260 --> 00:45:30.330
