00:45:57.060 --> 00:46:00.090
which we did as part of
the core bed delivery

00:46:00.090 --> 00:46:01.890
in January and February in the UEC team

00:46:01.890 --> 00:46:04.140
are reviewing that with
our communities team.

00:46:05.160 --> 00:46:06.570
And of course, the interesting challenge

00:46:06.570 --> 00:46:08.580
with community beds is
they're not all NHS beds.

00:46:08.580 --> 00:46:09.540
- [Richard] Yeah.

00:46:09.540 --> 00:46:12.240
- So it depends on how
local systems are managing

00:46:12.240 --> 00:46:15.270
that intersection between
NHS community beds

00:46:15.270 --> 00:46:17.610
and private sector.

00:46:17.610 --> 00:46:22.170
But we know that although
the data is less good

00:46:22.170 --> 00:46:25.890
and less complete to the
point that Mark was making,

00:46:25.890 --> 00:46:29.100
that there are approximately
20,000 community beds

00:46:29.100 --> 00:46:32.370
and the length of stay in
those is hugely variable.

00:46:32.370 --> 00:46:36.120
Much more so than the variation
within the acute trusts.

00:46:36.120 --> 00:46:37.860
And so we think there
must be an opportunity

00:46:37.860 --> 00:46:40.590
to be looking at how
systems are managing those,

00:46:40.590 --> 00:46:42.390
but because the data
flows are quite different,

00:46:42.390 --> 00:46:44.310
that's something we think we need to work

00:46:44.310 --> 00:46:46.200
with systems to deliver
rather than it being

00:46:46.200 --> 00:46:47.760
a single national initiative.

00:46:47.760 --> 00:46:50.040
But that will form part
of our system planning

00:46:50.040 --> 00:46:53.370
for capacity next winter.
