00:50:15.480 --> 00:50:19.200
So if we just look at the 78 weeks,

00:50:19.200 --> 00:50:20.460
which if I'm not careful,

00:50:20.460 --> 00:50:22.800
I'll use the current number
for, which is not published.

00:50:22.800 --> 00:50:27.220
But as we say in the
paper, the 78 week waits

00:50:28.380 --> 00:50:32.163
was a 14,000 in the
January published data.

00:50:33.420 --> 00:50:35.820
So if we then look at people

00:50:35.820 --> 00:50:39.270
who entered the waiting
list 78 weeks before that,

00:50:39.270 --> 00:50:41.880
which would've been the autumn of '22,

00:50:41.880 --> 00:50:44.700
all of those people other than 14,000

00:50:44.700 --> 00:50:47.040
will had their procedure.

00:50:47.040 --> 00:50:49.650
So the fact that it
stays in published data

00:50:49.650 --> 00:50:52.260
around the 7 1/2 million
or the 7.13 million

00:50:52.260 --> 00:50:57.096
in the acute data indicates
huge movements every month.

00:50:57.096 --> 00:50:58.620
And that's why it's actually

00:50:58.620 --> 00:50:59.790
helpful to look at the long waiters,

00:50:59.790 --> 00:51:01.500
'cause that's where you
can see the numbers moving

00:51:01.500 --> 00:51:04.410
in a different way than we
can on the total wait list.

00:51:04.410 --> 00:51:06.580
I think it has been noted that

00:51:07.440 --> 00:51:09.060
in the commentary on the planning guidance

00:51:09.060 --> 00:51:11.010
that we are asking systems now

00:51:11.010 --> 00:51:12.900
to make sure they are
focused on the management

00:51:12.900 --> 00:51:14.280
of the entire waiting list

00:51:14.280 --> 00:51:16.530
and not just in inverted commas 'narrowly

00:51:16.530 --> 00:51:18.230
focused' on the long wait.

00:51:18.230 --> 00:51:19.500
It's because we think that is where

00:51:19.500 --> 00:51:21.450
some of the productivity benefits will be.

00:51:21.450 --> 00:51:23.820
We must treat people who've been waiting

00:51:23.820 --> 00:51:26.850
this long for treatment
and we must make sure

00:51:26.850 --> 00:51:28.710
that we're looking at
the entire waiting list

00:51:28.710 --> 00:51:30.650
and allowing clinicians to make decisions

00:51:30.650 --> 00:51:32.550
about what's the right sequence

00:51:32.550 --> 00:51:36.663
and urgency of treatment at
all levels of the waiting list.

00:51:37.800 --> 00:51:39.753
- But in the spirit of the progress

00:51:39.753 --> 00:51:41.157
that you outlined earlier

00:51:41.157 --> 00:51:43.260
and that month on month improvement.

00:51:43.260 --> 00:51:44.910
- Yes.
- That number about

00:51:44.910 --> 00:51:49.230
people waiting has always
been roughly the same,

00:51:49.230 --> 00:51:51.750
but actually there is a
phenomenal amount of change

00:51:51.750 --> 00:51:52.583
taking place within there-
- Yes.

00:51:52.583 --> 00:51:55.350
- As people are going through, yeah.

00:51:55.350 --> 00:51:56.253
- Yes, exactly.

00:51:58.260 --> 00:51:59.143
- Richard, if I may-
- Yeah, please.

00:51:59.143 --> 00:52:02.430
- Everything that impresses me about

00:52:02.430 --> 00:52:04.170
the report that's come through.

00:52:04.170 --> 00:52:07.590
It's about 18 months ago I
think we were asked to agree

00:52:07.590 --> 00:52:09.660
an urgent and emergency care strategy

00:52:09.660 --> 00:52:11.613
that laid out a number of these things.

00:52:12.607 --> 00:52:16.260
And I think what we're now
seeing is that strategy working

00:52:16.260 --> 00:52:17.430
its way through.
- Yeah.

00:52:17.430 --> 00:52:20.280
- And I just think
there's a powerful lesson

00:52:20.280 --> 00:52:22.710
in setting out a strategic approach

00:52:22.710 --> 00:52:24.810
and then seeing it applied

00:52:24.810 --> 00:52:26.850
in a very kind of disciplined way.

00:52:26.850 --> 00:52:30.420
And we often talk about management grip,

00:52:30.420 --> 00:52:32.370
but I think there's a really good story

00:52:32.370 --> 00:52:34.860
about a strategy laid
out and then the grip

00:52:34.860 --> 00:52:38.375
and then the revisions in the
light of experience to that

00:52:38.375 --> 00:52:39.840
which is playing its way through.

00:52:39.840 --> 00:52:42.057
And yes, there is a lot more to do

00:52:42.057 --> 00:52:44.520
and we shouldn't lose sight of that,

00:52:44.520 --> 00:52:46.380
but credit where credit is due.

00:52:46.380 --> 00:52:51.380
There's a significant
record of marginal gains

00:52:52.200 --> 00:52:53.310
being expressed each month-
- Yeah.

00:52:53.310 --> 00:52:55.310
- Which is working its way through.

00:52:55.310 --> 00:52:57.899
So just in case, it's
worth pulling that out.

00:52:57.899 --> 00:52:59.809
- May I just make a
additional point, Richard?

00:52:59.809 --> 00:53:00.642
- Of course.

00:53:00.642 --> 00:53:02.790
- So thank you very much for saying that,

00:53:02.790 --> 00:53:07.200
and obviously, that massive
credit to Sarah-Jane

00:53:07.200 --> 00:53:09.600
and to Julian Redhead, the
clinical lead on that area,

00:53:09.600 --> 00:53:11.910
and lots of other people.

00:53:11.910 --> 00:53:14.370
The thing I hope people
would also recognise is,

00:53:14.370 --> 00:53:16.590
I think this is also is two

00:53:16.590 --> 00:53:18.420
additional things on top of what you said.

00:53:18.420 --> 00:53:20.411
The first is there's a
strategy which has been

00:53:20.411 --> 00:53:23.700
further developed by
actual usage on the ground

00:53:23.700 --> 00:53:25.298
and then amended in order to say, "Fine,

00:53:25.298 --> 00:53:27.861
that particular thing has
delivered this benefit.

00:53:27.861 --> 00:53:30.330
This has delivered much
more than we expect it to."

00:53:30.330 --> 00:53:32.250
And this one actually, it's fine,

00:53:32.250 --> 00:53:33.240
it's a marginal benefit,

00:53:33.240 --> 00:53:35.250
but it isn't the one that's
made the biggest difference.

00:53:35.250 --> 00:53:37.971
And so that regular
ability to review the data,

00:53:37.971 --> 00:53:40.560
learn and then reinforce that locally,

00:53:40.560 --> 00:53:41.520
I think it's been tremendous.

00:53:41.520 --> 00:53:44.490
And I think it's also a
good example of trying

00:53:44.490 --> 00:53:45.957
and we don't always get it 100%

00:53:45.957 --> 00:53:49.150
of making the interventions
at the right level

00:53:50.160 --> 00:53:52.110
so that there is a level of ownership

00:53:52.110 --> 00:53:53.547
at trust level or a system level.

00:53:53.547 --> 00:53:54.630
And some of the interventions,

00:53:54.630 --> 00:53:56.811
obviously, not just
within the acute system,

00:53:56.811 --> 00:53:59.279
some at ICB level, some at national level,

00:53:59.279 --> 00:54:02.460
and trying to get the
right balance of capability

00:54:02.460 --> 00:54:03.660
and capacity in the right place

00:54:03.660 --> 00:54:05.130
has been really critical to delivery,

00:54:05.130 --> 00:54:06.963
and there's more to more to do.

00:54:08.790 --> 00:54:09.769
- Thank you.

00:54:09.769 --> 00:54:12.810
I mean, Emily very good.

00:54:12.810 --> 00:54:13.643
Thank you very much.

00:54:13.643 --> 00:54:14.476
This gives us a good sense actually

00:54:14.476 --> 00:54:15.949
just for the operational scale.

00:54:15.949 --> 00:54:16.951
- [Emily] Yeah.

00:54:16.951 --> 00:54:21.951
- And the level of provision
the NHS is providing.

00:54:22.410 --> 00:54:23.790
That was also,

00:54:23.790 --> 00:54:25.620
it's worth saying very much current

00:54:25.620 --> 00:54:28.560
operational performance
and reflection on that.

00:54:28.560 --> 00:54:30.877
And we had a very good
conversation this morning

00:54:30.877 --> 00:54:34.650
on some of the longer
term enabling priorities.

00:54:34.650 --> 00:54:36.570
So the private board,

00:54:36.570 --> 00:54:38.640
we had an update again on
the new hospitals programme.

00:54:38.640 --> 00:54:41.610
We had an update on
long-term workforce plan.

00:54:41.610 --> 00:54:44.610
Julian gave us a sense for
a long-term capital plan,

00:54:44.610 --> 00:54:47.460
which is emerging strongly.

00:54:47.460 --> 00:54:49.980
And also with Vin, we
had a very good update

00:54:49.980 --> 00:54:51.720
on the transformation directorate,

00:54:51.720 --> 00:54:55.360
looking at whether it's the
NHS app or FDP or live services

00:54:56.859 --> 00:55:00.090
or data available for research

00:55:00.090 --> 00:55:02.070
or actually just digitalization
of the front line

00:55:02.070 --> 00:55:04.200
or digitalization of UEC.

00:55:04.200 --> 00:55:06.330
So actually, there's a huge amount here

00:55:06.330 --> 00:55:08.010
going on of a longer term,

00:55:08.010 --> 00:55:10.470
potentially transformational nature.

00:55:10.470 --> 00:55:11.640
In addition, actually Emily,

00:55:11.640 --> 00:55:13.710
to what you just portrayed for us

00:55:13.710 --> 00:55:15.900
in terms of just the sheer hard work

00:55:15.900 --> 00:55:19.830
of NHS staff actually providing
this level of service.

00:55:19.830 --> 00:55:20.930
So thank you for that.

00:55:22.050 --> 00:55:24.090
Julian, financial performance update.

00:55:24.090 --> 00:55:25.230
And do you want to do that

00:55:25.230 --> 00:55:27.810
and then also pick up
again from your perspective

00:55:27.810 --> 00:55:28.840
on planning guidance.

00:55:28.840 --> 00:55:30.750
- Sure.
- And budget.

00:55:30.750 --> 00:55:35.750
- So '23, '24, clearly, we
are in the final few days.

00:55:38.970 --> 00:55:41.290
I've said for a while that

00:55:42.750 --> 00:55:47.750
we are forecasting a system
overspend compared to plan

00:55:47.910 --> 00:55:51.903
of around a billion, just
over a billion pounds.

00:55:53.250 --> 00:55:57.483
Clearly, we've had challenges
throughout the year,

00:55:58.380 --> 00:56:03.180
in particular, the impact
of industrial action,

00:56:03.180 --> 00:56:06.030
but also the ongoing impact

00:56:06.030 --> 00:56:09.000
of higher inflation than that forecast.

00:56:09.000 --> 00:56:11.943
And from which we were originally funded,

00:56:13.470 --> 00:56:16.770
the government has provided extra funding

00:56:16.770 --> 00:56:19.740
to cover the impact of the strikes

00:56:19.740 --> 00:56:21.573
around 1.2 billion pounds.

00:56:23.640 --> 00:56:25.800
And we have reflected
that people could do less

00:56:25.800 --> 00:56:26.640
because we've lost time

00:56:26.640 --> 00:56:31.640
and we reduced the elective
activity target as a result.

00:56:32.250 --> 00:56:34.290
But the impact of inflation,

00:56:34.290 --> 00:56:37.440
the kind of recurrent impact
of higher inflation '22, '23

00:56:37.440 --> 00:56:38.970
running into this year,

00:56:38.970 --> 00:56:43.480
and then in '23, '24 where the governments

00:56:44.520 --> 00:56:46.863
or the latest data, the ONS data is that,

00:56:48.090 --> 00:56:50.878
inflation between '22, '23 is about 6.7%

00:56:50.878 --> 00:56:55.110
at the time the forecast
had been about 4.4

00:56:55.110 --> 00:56:57.972
and is about the same again this year

00:56:57.972 --> 00:57:00.777
against the forecast of
somewhere around three.

00:57:00.777 --> 00:57:02.850
And the combined financial impact of that

00:57:02.850 --> 00:57:06.900
is about 1.7 billion pounds as '23, '24.

00:57:06.900 --> 00:57:10.170
So the kind of the financial pressure

00:57:10.170 --> 00:57:12.870
has to be seen in that context.

00:57:12.870 --> 00:57:17.460
Nevertheless, for the
whole of the NHS mandate,

00:57:17.460 --> 00:57:20.343
we are forecasting to come in.

00:57:21.960 --> 00:57:25.440
I mean, broadly, I'm still
balanced probability confident

00:57:25.440 --> 00:57:28.200
we will come in under the budget.

00:57:28.200 --> 00:57:29.610
The official forecast in the paper

00:57:29.610 --> 00:57:32.460
shows a potential risk of
up to 90 million pounds

00:57:32.460 --> 00:57:37.460
over on 170 billion pound budget.

00:57:39.751 --> 00:57:41.040
So I mean, it is very tight.

00:57:41.040 --> 00:57:42.090
It is very tight.

00:57:42.090 --> 00:57:44.700
We've managed the pressure this year.

00:57:44.700 --> 00:57:47.700
Clearly, in some areas
we have done less than

00:57:47.700 --> 00:57:48.533
we would've hoped

00:57:48.533 --> 00:57:51.213
because of the financial
pressure in systems.

00:57:52.968 --> 00:57:56.613
The NHS England change
programme has basically,

00:57:58.050 --> 00:58:01.470
will have saved us this year
over 300 million pounds,

00:58:01.470 --> 00:58:05.940
which has gone to
balance the whole budget,

00:58:05.940 --> 00:58:08.670
but it is clearly very tight.

00:58:08.670 --> 00:58:12.240
And whether we come in
this year largely hinges

00:58:12.240 --> 00:58:15.600
on a couple of contractual
disputes out in the NHS.

00:58:15.600 --> 00:58:17.520
But look, I still think

00:58:17.520 --> 00:58:22.520
we will come in on budget
but just on budget.

00:58:22.800 --> 00:58:27.750
And on capital where the
total provider capital budget

00:58:27.750 --> 00:58:32.580
set by the department
is 7.4 billion pounds

00:58:32.580 --> 00:58:34.350
and we are currently
forecasting an underspend

00:58:34.350 --> 00:58:37.080
against that of 32 million pounds.

00:58:37.080 --> 00:58:42.080
So again, there and
thereabouts like on the money.

00:58:43.410 --> 00:58:47.190
So that sort of sets up
the context for '24, '25.

00:58:47.190 --> 00:58:50.610
As Amanda said, we formally published

00:58:50.610 --> 00:58:54.723
the guidance yesterday evening, yesterday.

00:58:57.300 --> 00:59:01.020
At the budget, the government
provided and we welcome,

00:59:01.020 --> 00:59:02.580
I mean, really welcome the extra funding

00:59:02.580 --> 00:59:06.180
the government provided
2.45 billion pounds.

00:59:06.180 --> 00:59:11.180
Largely, which covers the
recurrent cost of the pay deals,

00:59:11.550 --> 00:59:16.550
agreed and already
implemented over '23, '24.

00:59:21.000 --> 00:59:24.090
It also means that certain
things we might have had to do,

00:59:24.090 --> 00:59:24.923
we don't have to do.

00:59:24.923 --> 00:59:26.610
And it means we can keep going with,

00:59:26.610 --> 00:59:29.490
for example, the investment
in improvement resources

00:59:29.490 --> 00:59:32.670
to support GP practises.

00:59:32.670 --> 00:59:34.200
It means we can keep going

00:59:34.200 --> 00:59:36.690
with the investment in maternity services

00:59:36.690 --> 00:59:39.660
where we are putting in 180
million pounds, the next year,

00:59:39.660 --> 00:59:43.023
in fact slightly more
than 180 million pounds.

00:59:43.980 --> 00:59:48.980
It means that we are still absolutely

00:59:49.050 --> 00:59:51.570
requiring systems to deliver

00:59:51.570 --> 00:59:55.530
on the mental health
investment standard next year.

00:59:55.530 --> 00:59:57.810
It means we can keep going
with the extra investment

00:59:57.810 --> 01:00:02.220
into the pharmacy services
as we seek to relieve

01:00:02.220 --> 01:00:04.998
some of the pressure on GP practises

01:00:04.998 --> 01:00:06.720
over the course of next year

01:00:06.720 --> 01:00:08.220
and provide better access,

01:00:08.220 --> 01:00:11.853
better service to the public.

01:00:12.750 --> 01:00:15.850
But in the round, total NHS funding

01:00:16.740 --> 01:00:20.160
is next year forecast
to be flat in real time,

01:00:20.160 --> 01:00:24.460
slightly better than flat
but like 0.25% better

01:00:26.070 --> 01:00:27.360
than sort of flat reel,

01:00:27.360 --> 01:00:31.653
better than just staying
in line with inflation.

01:00:32.790 --> 01:00:34.980
Clearly, some things are
rising faster than inflation,

01:00:34.980 --> 01:00:36.497
which means we are,

01:00:36.497 --> 01:00:39.000
there is a squeeze.
- Yeah.

01:00:39.000 --> 01:00:41.460
- So as we look ahead at next year,

01:00:41.460 --> 01:00:44.880
the planning guidance
sets out that despite that

01:00:44.880 --> 01:00:49.650
we are still seeking
to improve performance.

01:00:49.650 --> 01:00:52.800
It will not be a step change
but we will still seek

01:00:52.800 --> 01:00:55.140
to keep on building on the
progress we've made this year

01:00:55.140 --> 01:01:00.140
on four hour performance,
ambulance performance.

01:01:01.200 --> 01:01:02.790
We will continue to drive

01:01:02.790 --> 01:01:06.090
the virtual elimination of 65 week waits

01:01:06.090 --> 01:01:10.830
and as Emily said, can we continue to hold

01:01:10.830 --> 01:01:13.335
and improve the position
on the total waiting list?

01:01:13.335 --> 01:01:14.730
- [Emily] Yeah.

01:01:14.730 --> 01:01:19.730
- We will keep going on
expanding mental health services,

01:01:19.980 --> 01:01:23.640
improving access in primary care,

01:01:23.640 --> 01:01:25.260
but this is not a step change.

01:01:25.260 --> 01:01:26.910
This is very much a consolidation.

01:01:26.910 --> 01:01:29.610
We have put significant investment in

01:01:29.610 --> 01:01:32.640
over the course of the last
two years into diagnostics,

01:01:32.640 --> 01:01:36.150
elective capacity, core beds.

01:01:36.150 --> 01:01:37.781
This is not a year where we're seeing,

01:01:37.781 --> 01:01:40.380
we will see a further
big increase in capacity.

01:01:40.380 --> 01:01:44.586
This is about consolidation
and now really driving,

01:01:44.586 --> 01:01:49.113
the best value out of those services.

01:01:49.950 --> 01:01:54.570
Now we have seen an increase

01:01:54.570 --> 01:01:56.793
in substantive staffing this year.

01:01:58.080 --> 01:01:59.850
We are beginning to see

01:01:59.850 --> 01:02:02.100
therefore a reduction in
temporary staffing costs.

01:02:02.100 --> 01:02:04.830
We're forecasting to
spend 400 million pounds

01:02:04.830 --> 01:02:09.830
less in '23, '24 on agency staff

01:02:09.870 --> 01:02:12.193
compared to the previous year.

01:02:12.193 --> 01:02:14.880
And we will need to see,

01:02:14.880 --> 01:02:16.860
and we are seeing I think

01:02:16.860 --> 01:02:18.020
sort of an underlying improvement

01:02:18.020 --> 01:02:20.280
in productivity this year.

01:02:20.280 --> 01:02:21.570
If you look at the aggregate numbers,

01:02:21.570 --> 01:02:22.530
it doesn't look that different,

01:02:22.530 --> 01:02:24.780
but you just have to take account of

01:02:24.780 --> 01:02:26.700
the kind of level of strike action-

01:02:26.700 --> 01:02:28.230
- Yeah.
- That we have had.

01:02:28.230 --> 01:02:30.210
And if we hadn't had that strike action,

01:02:30.210 --> 01:02:33.840
we would've seen material
improvement in productivity,

01:02:33.840 --> 01:02:35.670
I think this year.

01:02:35.670 --> 01:02:39.630
So next year is consolidate,

01:02:39.630 --> 01:02:44.295
keep driving out the costs
of temporary staffing

01:02:44.295 --> 01:02:47.340
where we are looking
for at least the same,

01:02:47.340 --> 01:02:49.290
again in terms of savings.

01:02:49.290 --> 01:02:50.820
So at least another 400 million.

01:02:50.820 --> 01:02:52.680
But really, I think we
will need to go further

01:02:52.680 --> 01:02:55.650
than that in '24, '25.

01:02:55.650 --> 01:02:58.410
We need every provider to really review

01:02:58.410 --> 01:03:01.260
where extra resource has gone in

01:03:01.260 --> 01:03:02.698
and make sure it's appropriate

01:03:02.698 --> 01:03:07.410
and we are getting the best value for it.

01:03:07.410 --> 01:03:10.260
Whilst we, as I said,
seek to just continue

01:03:10.260 --> 01:03:14.730
to improve aggregate performance.

01:03:14.730 --> 01:03:18.570
And then clearly, we are
still looking to hold onto

01:03:18.570 --> 01:03:20.220
the investments and plans we are making

01:03:20.220 --> 01:03:22.140
into the long-term workforce plan,

01:03:22.140 --> 01:03:24.870
the continued investment
in nursing, medical,

01:03:24.870 --> 01:03:27.060
and other clinical training places

01:03:27.060 --> 01:03:29.820
over the course of this next year,

01:03:29.820 --> 01:03:32.730
as well as the investment to keep going

01:03:32.730 --> 01:03:36.240
with frontline digitization programme,

01:03:36.240 --> 01:03:38.130
the roll out of the
federated data platform,

01:03:38.130 --> 01:03:39.960
the development of the NHS app.

01:03:39.960 --> 01:03:44.960
And we are looking ahead to the invest,

01:03:45.240 --> 01:03:47.460
how we deploy and get the best value

01:03:47.460 --> 01:03:48.750
for the investment announced

01:03:48.750 --> 01:03:50.940
by the chancellor at the budget,

01:03:50.940 --> 01:03:54.120
the 3.4 billion pounds into
technology enabled change,

01:03:54.120 --> 01:03:56.700
which is a core component of

01:03:56.700 --> 01:03:58.740
how we deliver improved productivity

01:03:58.740 --> 01:04:01.590
in a sustainable NHS
over the next parliament.

01:04:01.590 --> 01:04:05.040
And we will set out more details of that

01:04:05.040 --> 01:04:07.440
later in the summer.

01:04:07.440 --> 01:04:09.780
So Richard, I will finish there.

01:04:09.780 --> 01:04:14.780
- So comments or questions,
reactions for Julian?

01:04:16.320 --> 01:04:17.730
Wol.
- Thanks.

01:04:17.730 --> 01:04:18.563
Thanks Julian.

01:04:18.563 --> 01:04:20.070
As ever it's hugely complicated

01:04:20.070 --> 01:04:23.580
and a lot of hard work
to get to the numbers,

01:04:23.580 --> 01:04:26.331
and of course, the system
going forward is pressured.

01:04:26.331 --> 01:04:31.080
The 3.4 billion which was
announced in the budget is,

01:04:31.080 --> 01:04:32.820
as I think Amanda said, transformational.

01:04:32.820 --> 01:04:35.543
I think I really believe that
it's very, very good news.

01:04:36.570 --> 01:04:38.190
I mean maybe you, Julian, perhaps Vin,

01:04:38.190 --> 01:04:40.800
just comment on how we see that money

01:04:40.800 --> 01:04:44.850
being sort of dispersed over
time and into what activities

01:04:44.850 --> 01:04:46.110
because not all of it's going to be on

01:04:46.110 --> 01:04:48.287
the sort of shiny stuff
that people will see.

01:04:48.287 --> 01:04:49.500
- It's so important.
- It's really important.

01:04:49.500 --> 01:04:50.580
We recognise there's quite a lot of

01:04:50.580 --> 01:04:52.680
backbone stuff to do as well.

01:04:52.680 --> 01:04:53.820
- Should we get Vin to start,

01:04:53.820 --> 01:04:55.890
Julian, if you want to
come in and support Vin,

01:04:55.890 --> 01:04:57.300
do you want to take that?

01:04:57.300 --> 01:04:58.133
- Yeah, thanks Wol.

01:04:58.133 --> 01:04:58.966
So in the budget,

01:04:58.966 --> 01:05:00.960
I think the Chancellor
announced three chunks.

01:05:00.960 --> 01:05:04.560
The 3.6 billion is divided into firstly,

01:05:04.560 --> 01:05:06.990
more than half of it is directed towards

01:05:06.990 --> 01:05:10.310
improving the basic underlying
infrastructure of the NHS.

01:05:10.310 --> 01:05:13.320
So that ranges from everything from

01:05:13.320 --> 01:05:16.200
improving the underlying
broadband infrastructure

01:05:16.200 --> 01:05:19.710
and bandwidth so on, to providing new,

01:05:19.710 --> 01:05:21.180
what we call end user devices,

01:05:21.180 --> 01:05:23.670
but actually devices that staff can use.

01:05:23.670 --> 01:05:26.940
For example, computers on
wards or in outpatient clinics

01:05:26.940 --> 01:05:29.520
or in GP surgeries, handheld devices.

01:05:29.520 --> 01:05:31.530
So you can enter data actually

01:05:31.530 --> 01:05:34.080
while you're standing next to the patient.

01:05:34.080 --> 01:05:36.270
There's about another 450 million

01:05:36.270 --> 01:05:37.917
that's provided for further
development of the app.

01:05:37.917 --> 01:05:39.420
The chairs already talked about

01:05:39.420 --> 01:05:41.187
the transformational impact of the app

01:05:41.187 --> 01:05:43.230
and some of the plans that we have both

01:05:43.230 --> 01:05:46.410
to build further on its existing features

01:05:46.410 --> 01:05:47.850
to help patients navigate

01:05:47.850 --> 01:05:50.880
their way around the health
system, organise appointments,

01:05:50.880 --> 01:05:55.450
rearrange them, order
prescriptions, and access NHS 111,

01:05:57.360 --> 01:06:00.810
and also to introduce a new
digital health check into it.

01:06:00.810 --> 01:06:03.330
And then the remaining amount is

01:06:03.330 --> 01:06:05.580
for upgrading our data infrastructure.

01:06:05.580 --> 01:06:08.640
The federated data platform
is one example of that,

01:06:08.640 --> 01:06:11.430
but we also want to
improve faster data flows

01:06:11.430 --> 01:06:13.927
that will improve the way that information

01:06:13.927 --> 01:06:16.050
is travels around the NHS.

01:06:16.050 --> 01:06:18.900
For example, both to
support direct clinical care

01:06:18.900 --> 01:06:21.990
but also to support the reporting of data.

01:06:21.990 --> 01:06:23.820
For example, for the
purposes of this board

01:06:23.820 --> 01:06:27.003
in order that we can organise
care most effectively.

01:06:27.840 --> 01:06:29.250
There is money also in the budget.

01:06:29.250 --> 01:06:30.840
Finally for innovation,

01:06:30.840 --> 01:06:32.490
there's two specific examples

01:06:32.490 --> 01:06:34.350
that are called out of AI tools

01:06:34.350 --> 01:06:36.240
that can support productivity,

01:06:36.240 --> 01:06:38.851
making it easier for
clinicians to do tasks

01:06:38.851 --> 01:06:41.970
to enable them and release
more time for care.

01:06:41.970 --> 01:06:43.590
But there will be other innovations

01:06:43.590 --> 01:06:46.560
within that package of
things that are not set out.

01:06:46.560 --> 01:06:48.330
The job of the next year that we've got

01:06:48.330 --> 01:06:51.140
is actually to put details onto that plan

01:06:51.140 --> 01:06:53.880
and we will be very clearly setting out

01:06:53.880 --> 01:06:56.130
the key milestones that we will follow

01:06:56.130 --> 01:06:57.480
over the course of that,

01:06:57.480 --> 01:06:59.940
how we will gain productivity
benefits out of it,

01:06:59.940 --> 01:07:02.610
and most importantly how staff
will actually see benefits

01:07:02.610 --> 01:07:05.430
in real life that will make
their working lives easier.

01:07:05.430 --> 01:07:09.270
- Wol, I mean, I would just
add to your phraseology,

01:07:09.270 --> 01:07:12.063
I mean I agree it is
transformational actually

01:07:12.063 --> 01:07:15.510
'cause that's one of the other reflections

01:07:15.510 --> 01:07:17.130
and perhaps I can do it,

01:07:17.130 --> 01:07:19.800
although I know a number of
the board colleagues share it.

01:07:19.800 --> 01:07:23.164
I mean, as an old finance
director from the private sector,

01:07:24.300 --> 01:07:29.300
I always delighted at the
ability you have Julian

01:07:31.110 --> 01:07:34.500
with your team and through
the systems engagement

01:07:34.500 --> 01:07:37.770
at managing what is a clear,
very, very sizable budget

01:07:37.770 --> 01:07:42.533
and you manage it really
well to come in as close to

01:07:42.533 --> 01:07:44.160
and but really close to budget.

01:07:44.160 --> 01:07:46.200
I mean, my ability to
do that in my old world,

01:07:46.200 --> 01:07:50.283
I have to admit I had
nothing like your skillset.

01:07:51.450 --> 01:07:53.160
But one of the ways it happens, of course,

01:07:53.160 --> 01:07:56.550
actually is some pretty stark decisioning

01:07:56.550 --> 01:07:58.350
you have to make with the executives.

01:07:58.350 --> 01:08:00.960
So for instance, when late last
year with having to look at

01:08:00.960 --> 01:08:04.170
how we fund the cost of
the industrial action.

01:08:04.170 --> 01:08:06.270
In the end, there's
contributions from treasury,

01:08:06.270 --> 01:08:08.708
there's contribution to
the department of health

01:08:08.708 --> 01:08:11.070
and then we make our contributions,

01:08:11.070 --> 01:08:13.320
one of which actually used was last year

01:08:13.320 --> 01:08:15.630
actually slowing down significantly

01:08:15.630 --> 01:08:20.100
or stopping a number of
technology investments which are,

01:08:20.100 --> 01:08:22.140
it's a very necessary
shorter term measure,

01:08:22.140 --> 01:08:23.850
but actually for the longer term,

01:08:23.850 --> 01:08:26.160
if we're going to transform,
we've got to stay steady.

01:08:26.160 --> 01:08:29.313
And why I think it is
transformation isn't just the sum,

01:08:30.150 --> 01:08:32.109
it isn't just the fact actually that

01:08:32.109 --> 01:08:35.080
the sum derived actually
from very good presentations,

01:08:35.080 --> 01:08:36.780
Amanda, by you and Julian

01:08:36.780 --> 01:08:38.610
and essentially into the treasury

01:08:38.610 --> 01:08:40.020
around where would we spend it,

01:08:40.020 --> 01:08:41.550
what would we ask for and so on.

01:08:41.550 --> 01:08:43.620
Which now needs to be delivered

01:08:43.620 --> 01:08:45.210
in more detail with milestones.

01:08:45.210 --> 01:08:49.590
But actually, we painted the
picture very, very clearly,

01:08:49.590 --> 01:08:51.750
but actually, it's
effectively ring fenced,

01:08:51.750 --> 01:08:54.930
it's a commitment around
a technology spend

01:08:54.930 --> 01:08:56.340
which we can carry on with

01:08:56.340 --> 01:08:58.680
and that will have
transformational benefit.

01:08:58.680 --> 01:08:59.880
Whereas in fact in this last year,

01:08:59.880 --> 01:09:01.830
one of the things you had to do

01:09:01.830 --> 01:09:03.930
was actually pull back quite suddenly

01:09:03.930 --> 01:09:07.470
late on in the year to
help us meet the budget.

01:09:07.470 --> 01:09:09.000
And one of the areas you had to go to

01:09:09.000 --> 01:09:12.456
was actually effectively technology spend.

01:09:12.456 --> 01:09:14.880
Whether we'd have spent it
all anyway is different.

01:09:14.880 --> 01:09:16.796
But actually, we certainly
stopped the progression

01:09:16.796 --> 01:09:18.750
of the spend or delayed

01:09:18.750 --> 01:09:21.870
in a number of projects
that those spend levels.

01:09:21.870 --> 01:09:23.231
So I think it's both the amount,

01:09:23.231 --> 01:09:25.800
it's the fact we painted a picture

01:09:25.800 --> 01:09:28.350
for government of where
we would like to deploy it

01:09:28.350 --> 01:09:30.270
and also it's the fact
that it's now ring fenced.

01:09:30.270 --> 01:09:34.830
So it goes alongside other, I
think longer term changes made

01:09:34.830 --> 01:09:37.290
and commitments made in our prioritisation

01:09:37.290 --> 01:09:38.670
like the long term,

01:09:38.670 --> 01:09:40.590
essentially the long term workforce plan.

01:09:40.590 --> 01:09:43.650
And here, with the technology
committed ring-fenced amount.

01:09:43.650 --> 01:09:46.110
We can put that alongside it
and really start to think about

01:09:46.110 --> 01:09:50.160
how the NHS will transform
and change going forward

01:09:50.160 --> 01:09:51.150
over the medium term.

01:09:51.150 --> 01:09:51.983
It's really good.

01:09:51.983 --> 01:09:53.250
- Can I have just one one plea,

01:09:53.250 --> 01:09:56.310
that's the disbursement
side maybe this is Julian,

01:09:56.310 --> 01:09:58.770
it's that, and I know it's
not entirely our control,

01:09:58.770 --> 01:10:01.620
but I'm slightly speaking
to other interested parties.

01:10:01.620 --> 01:10:05.850
If we can make the approval process

01:10:05.850 --> 01:10:08.340
actually meet the ambition
of what we're trying to do,

01:10:08.340 --> 01:10:11.250
which is slicker more agile,

01:10:11.250 --> 01:10:14.640
really focused on getting
the right answer quickly,

01:10:14.640 --> 01:10:15.544
that'd be helpful.

01:10:15.544 --> 01:10:16.377
And I don't know whether Julian,

01:10:16.377 --> 01:10:17.210
you have any comments on that,

01:10:17.210 --> 01:10:19.443
but it's quite complicated
as we have it currently.

01:10:20.700 --> 01:10:23.640
- Yeah, look, I'll just pick
up on a couple of things.

01:10:23.640 --> 01:10:25.803
First of all, as Vin has said,

01:10:26.640 --> 01:10:29.490
a lot of the investment we will put in

01:10:29.490 --> 01:10:31.980
just needs to go into making
the basics really work well

01:10:31.980 --> 01:10:34.710
and create a foundation upon
which you can then build

01:10:34.710 --> 01:10:37.467
the stuff which enables some of

01:10:37.467 --> 01:10:39.780
the kind of more exciting
service transformation,

01:10:39.780 --> 01:10:41.370
but it's actually how
do we just make it work?

01:10:41.370 --> 01:10:45.420
- Boring plumbing.
- Or you know, for

01:10:45.420 --> 01:10:48.390
actually our doctors, our nurses,

01:10:48.390 --> 01:10:51.097
all of the staff in the NHS.

01:10:52.887 --> 01:10:55.210
The second thing just to say is

01:10:56.520 --> 01:10:59.340
as we were having to do things

01:10:59.340 --> 01:11:01.980
to balance all the pressures last year,

01:11:01.980 --> 01:11:04.530
clearly, those are decisions
we are having to make

01:11:04.530 --> 01:11:06.090
with government agreed-
- Indeed.

01:11:06.090 --> 01:11:07.530
- With government about how we deal with-

01:11:07.530 --> 01:11:09.210
- Yeah.
- Those pressures

01:11:09.210 --> 01:11:12.360
and what is sort of
funded by the treasury,

01:11:12.360 --> 01:11:14.490
what compromises having to
be made within the department

01:11:14.490 --> 01:11:15.930
as well as what compromises

01:11:15.930 --> 01:11:18.510
are having to be made within the NHS.

01:11:18.510 --> 01:11:20.680
Those aren't just our call

01:11:21.750 --> 01:11:26.370
and on the sort of
decision making process.

01:11:26.370 --> 01:11:30.760
I mean, it's always
hard because inevitably

01:11:32.280 --> 01:11:34.710
the government, the treasury
are putting a lot of money

01:11:34.710 --> 01:11:38.380
into things and they want
to know those things are-

01:11:38.380 --> 01:11:41.312
- Well spent.
- Well spent.

01:11:41.312 --> 01:11:42.960
And they've got as much as reassurance

01:11:42.960 --> 01:11:46.380
as you can do, it's the
things they always planned for

01:11:46.380 --> 01:11:47.520
and they're going to get the outcome

01:11:47.520 --> 01:11:50.370
that they want on behalf of taxpayers.

01:11:50.370 --> 01:11:54.690
So I mean, in one sense
government is big inevitably,

01:11:54.690 --> 01:11:57.510
there are going to be quite
a lot of people involved.

01:11:57.510 --> 01:11:59.730
We are working as hard as we can,

01:11:59.730 --> 01:12:01.413
be it in the new hospital programme or the

01:12:01.413 --> 01:12:03.870
technology programmes,
or whatever it might be,

01:12:03.870 --> 01:12:05.820
just to create smooth processes,

01:12:05.820 --> 01:12:08.490
really effective relationships

01:12:08.490 --> 01:12:10.500
with our colleagues in the department,

01:12:10.500 --> 01:12:12.513
the treasury and the cabinet office.

01:12:14.100 --> 01:12:15.792
- I'm going to bring Ruth in a moment,

01:12:15.792 --> 01:12:18.833
Emily just something I
think you were sharing

01:12:18.833 --> 01:12:19.980
with us this morning.

01:12:19.980 --> 01:12:21.753
It goes to to Julian's theme about

01:12:21.753 --> 01:12:23.880
of a consolidation year,

01:12:23.880 --> 01:12:26.730
but actually, the significant
investment that we've received

01:12:26.730 --> 01:12:29.850
and had has been deployed
into diagnostic centres,

01:12:29.850 --> 01:12:31.770
community diagnostic centres.

01:12:31.770 --> 01:12:34.230
So that as far as we can
see it, that trajectory,

01:12:34.230 --> 01:12:35.820
but we've built, we built out haven't we?

01:12:35.820 --> 01:12:38.100
I think, much more significant.

01:12:38.100 --> 01:12:40.230
We've built out more diagnostic capacity

01:12:40.230 --> 01:12:42.810
whether we've still got enough.

01:12:42.810 --> 01:12:43.860
But actually, we've built out

01:12:43.860 --> 01:12:45.240
significant diagnostic capacity.

01:12:45.240 --> 01:12:47.490
We just lifted quite
substantially, I think,

01:12:47.490 --> 01:12:50.130
the number of diagnostic
interventions carried out

01:12:50.130 --> 01:12:52.705
over the last 18 months
or so, is that right?

01:12:52.705 --> 01:12:56.730
- Yes, that's the 113%
increase year on year

01:12:56.730 --> 01:12:57.720
that I mentioned

01:12:57.720 --> 01:13:01.800
and the 2.34 million diagnostic
events in January '24.

01:13:01.800 --> 01:13:06.800
And there are currently 156 CDCs operating

01:13:07.020 --> 01:13:10.392
and should be 159 by next week.

01:13:10.392 --> 01:13:15.030
And those have been carefully
located working with ICBs

01:13:15.030 --> 01:13:16.890
to try and address some of the disparities

01:13:16.890 --> 01:13:19.803
in accessibility of the hospital sites.

01:13:20.790 --> 01:13:22.830
We don't yet have data to show exactly

01:13:22.830 --> 01:13:24.960
how that's played through,

01:13:24.960 --> 01:13:28.620
but we will do that evaluation
over the next few months.

01:13:28.620 --> 01:13:29.670
- Very good, thank you.

01:13:29.670 --> 01:13:30.626
Ruth, you want to come in, I think.

01:13:30.626 --> 01:13:31.700
- Yeah, thank you.

01:13:31.700 --> 01:13:33.600
So I welcome the plan,

01:13:33.600 --> 01:13:37.560
and even Amanda has put
her name to the forward.

01:13:37.560 --> 01:13:41.040
Julian is presenting it,
actually this is a document

01:13:41.040 --> 01:13:43.980
that speaks to every member of the board.

01:13:43.980 --> 01:13:45.450
So I know that chief nurses,

01:13:45.450 --> 01:13:48.480
medical directors will be
reading this in detail.

01:13:48.480 --> 01:13:52.020
So I very much welcome the
first point in the priorities,

01:13:52.020 --> 01:13:53.010
the first bullet point

01:13:53.010 --> 01:13:55.710
where it talks about the
importance of quality,

01:13:55.710 --> 01:13:58.200
and of course, particularly in maternity

01:13:58.200 --> 01:14:01.107
and neonatal services and
seeing that throughout the plan,

01:14:01.107 --> 01:14:05.910
ensuring we continue the delivery
plan is really important.

01:14:05.910 --> 01:14:07.590
And then of course, it's
got lots of great things

01:14:07.590 --> 01:14:10.740
in about a women's hub in every ICB,

01:14:10.740 --> 01:14:12.810
a focus on children, young people,

01:14:12.810 --> 01:14:15.750
lots of equality,
diversity, and inclusion.

01:14:15.750 --> 01:14:17.700
Martha's Rule is in there-
- Yeah.

01:14:17.700 --> 01:14:20.490
- Which I'm really
pleased to see plus more.

01:14:20.490 --> 01:14:24.060
And then Julian, in that one long table,

01:14:24.060 --> 01:14:28.770
I do genuinely back the plan
to reduce the agency spend,

01:14:28.770 --> 01:14:31.200
particularly in my profession
but also in medical.

01:14:31.200 --> 01:14:32.610
I know Steve would back that too,

01:14:32.610 --> 01:14:35.040
as well as other professionals
getting that down.

01:14:35.040 --> 01:14:38.340
Particularly, now, we've
got more substantive staff

01:14:38.340 --> 01:14:41.505
does need to continue to
be a real focus this year.

01:14:41.505 --> 01:14:42.529
Thank you.

01:14:42.529 --> 01:14:43.890
- Thank you.

01:14:43.890 --> 01:14:45.012
Vin, do you want to come in?

01:14:45.012 --> 01:14:45.845
- Thanks chair.

01:14:45.845 --> 01:14:47.670
So just going back to technology,

01:14:47.670 --> 01:14:50.147
I think it's probably just
worth saying three things.

01:14:50.147 --> 01:14:53.310
So the first thing is I
think even over the last year

01:14:53.310 --> 01:14:55.770
we have hit all of our
commitments on technology.

01:14:55.770 --> 01:14:59.870
So for example, we hit the
target for 90% of trusts

01:14:59.870 --> 01:15:02.100
to have an electronic patient record

01:15:02.100 --> 01:15:05.250
ahead of schedule in
December of this year,

01:15:05.250 --> 01:15:07.950
still got 10% to go, which
is a significant number.

01:15:07.950 --> 01:15:10.170
But getting ahead of
schedule that we agreed

01:15:10.170 --> 01:15:12.150
with government is really significant.

01:15:12.150 --> 01:15:15.450
We're ahead of our target
for the number of patients

01:15:15.450 --> 01:15:18.060
who are downloading the NHS App.

01:15:18.060 --> 01:15:20.220
So you've got the NHS App in your pocket

01:15:20.220 --> 01:15:22.950
and Pharmacy First required
a significant amount of work

01:15:22.950 --> 01:15:26.070
to connect community
pharmacies to the GP record

01:15:26.070 --> 01:15:26.934
so that the consultations

01:15:26.934 --> 01:15:29.370
that pharmacists are carrying
out can be connected in.

01:15:29.370 --> 01:15:30.930
And so I think that is very important

01:15:30.930 --> 01:15:34.110
that the technology teams have
done a lot in the last year,

01:15:34.110 --> 01:15:36.120
we've not been standing still.

01:15:36.120 --> 01:15:37.620
And if you look forward to the next year,

01:15:37.620 --> 01:15:39.240
because the money that
the Chancellor's announced

01:15:39.240 --> 01:15:42.030
doesn't flow till '25, '26,

01:15:42.030 --> 01:15:42.960
but over the next year

01:15:42.960 --> 01:15:44.970
there's some really
significant commitments

01:15:44.970 --> 01:15:46.260
to technology investment

01:15:46.260 --> 01:15:48.600
in the operational planning guidance

01:15:48.600 --> 01:15:51.450
and we'll crack on and be
delivering those in the next year.

01:15:51.450 --> 01:15:53.850
But in the preparation for the '25, '26,

01:15:53.850 --> 01:15:58.050
we will also be doing, as
deputy chair is indicated,

01:15:58.050 --> 01:15:59.040
as Julian talked about,

01:15:59.040 --> 01:16:01.530
working closely with
colleagues in government

01:16:01.530 --> 01:16:03.900
to make sure that we are absolutely ready

01:16:03.900 --> 01:16:05.220
to hit the ground running

01:16:05.220 --> 01:16:09.033
and turbocharge our investment
in technology from '25, '26.

01:16:09.943 --> 01:16:10.830
- Emily.

01:16:10.830 --> 01:16:11.663
- Just a really quick point

01:16:11.663 --> 01:16:13.511
'cause of what Vin just said reminded me

01:16:13.511 --> 01:16:16.350
about the answer to Mark's question.

01:16:16.350 --> 01:16:18.810
As trusts have been
implementing EPR systems,

01:16:18.810 --> 01:16:20.190
they've paused reporting-
- Yeah.

01:16:20.190 --> 01:16:23.400
- On RTT in order to
move to the new system.

01:16:23.400 --> 01:16:26.340
So that will be what that
chart was referring to.

01:16:26.340 --> 01:16:27.173
Thank you.

01:16:28.712 --> 01:16:29.545
- Good answer.

01:16:31.170 --> 01:16:33.146
Very fast footwork, Emily, if I may say.

01:16:38.786 --> 01:16:41.040
Julian, is there anything else you'd want

01:16:41.040 --> 01:16:44.610
to add either around budget
or planning guidance?

01:16:44.610 --> 01:16:46.110
We've got financial
performance for the year.

01:16:46.110 --> 01:16:47.960
- I don't think so, no.
- Very good.

01:16:48.930 --> 01:16:50.190
Should we move on?

01:16:50.190 --> 01:16:52.830
Okay, let's go to next item.

01:16:52.830 --> 01:16:54.840
It's item six, I think on the agenda.

01:16:54.840 --> 01:16:56.823
So ADHD,

01:16:57.750 --> 01:17:01.020
Claire, you and Catherine
I think are going to

01:17:01.020 --> 01:17:03.000
walk us through this or
draw out the main points.

01:17:03.000 --> 01:17:04.375
I mean, it's a very good paper.

01:17:04.375 --> 01:17:06.900
I mean, obviously, assume it's been read

01:17:06.900 --> 01:17:08.460
but if you can draw out the main points,

01:17:08.460 --> 01:17:09.293
that'd be very helpful.

01:17:09.293 --> 01:17:11.280
- And Steve's going to just-

01:17:11.280 --> 01:17:12.300
- Oh, is it Steve?
- Introduce.

01:17:12.300 --> 01:17:13.628
- That was a hospital pass.
- Sorry, Steve.

01:17:14.940 --> 01:17:16.290
Just being corrected by Amanda.

01:17:16.290 --> 01:17:18.188
So Steve, if you'd like to start-

01:17:18.188 --> 01:17:19.021
- I'm going to pass the mic.

01:17:19.021 --> 01:17:19.893
- And then we bring Claire and Catherine.

01:17:19.893 --> 01:17:21.486
- Don't worry.

01:17:21.486 --> 01:17:22.740
- Look, Richard,

01:17:22.740 --> 01:17:24.360
Chair, thank you very, very much

01:17:24.360 --> 01:17:27.000
for the time on the agenda today.

01:17:27.000 --> 01:17:29.220
Hopefully, the paper is
pretty self-explanatory,

01:17:29.220 --> 01:17:32.850
but just to try and give a
bit of a frame as to the why,

01:17:32.850 --> 01:17:35.250
why this is here, why now,

01:17:35.250 --> 01:17:38.013
and a bit about the
work that's being done.

01:17:39.570 --> 01:17:41.190
I think a number of colleagues

01:17:41.190 --> 01:17:43.170
have been involved in this work

01:17:43.170 --> 01:17:45.300
and I think we're really clear

01:17:45.300 --> 01:17:49.260
that this is a really important
issue for us to lean into.

01:17:49.260 --> 01:17:51.839
ADHD can have a really
quite profound impact

01:17:51.839 --> 01:17:55.440
on many aspects of people's lives

01:17:55.440 --> 01:17:59.580
and their life chances,
whether that's in academic,

01:17:59.580 --> 01:18:02.070
the academic arena, whether it's in work

01:18:02.070 --> 01:18:04.743
or broader social functioning.

01:18:06.360 --> 01:18:10.350
People living with ADHD and
their families deserve a caring

01:18:10.350 --> 01:18:13.650
and effective set of support from the NHS,

01:18:13.650 --> 01:18:16.920
but also more broadly across wider society

01:18:16.920 --> 01:18:18.842
and wider sectors.

01:18:18.842 --> 01:18:21.360
And I think hopefully
what colleagues will see

01:18:21.360 --> 01:18:23.430
from the work that's been done so far

01:18:23.430 --> 01:18:28.430
and in the paper that for
the NHS' part in this,

01:18:29.280 --> 01:18:32.523
we're really determined
to play that really well.

01:18:34.020 --> 01:18:35.580
There isn't a cure for ADHD,

01:18:35.580 --> 01:18:39.870
but it is possible to
help manage the problems

01:18:39.870 --> 01:18:43.050
that people living with ADHD face.

01:18:43.050 --> 01:18:47.520
There is obviously medication
which has been the area

01:18:47.520 --> 01:18:50.790
of particular intensive
focus more recently

01:18:50.790 --> 01:18:52.770
with some of the supply challenges.

01:18:52.770 --> 01:18:57.000
But there's also much
more broad support advice

01:18:57.000 --> 01:19:00.093
and non-therapeutic
interventions that are available.

01:19:01.620 --> 01:19:03.450
As I think colleagues are aware,

01:19:03.450 --> 01:19:06.750
NHS providers have been
reporting an increase

01:19:06.750 --> 01:19:09.630
in demand for a variety of reasons,

01:19:09.630 --> 01:19:12.090
including increased awareness.

01:19:12.090 --> 01:19:15.167
And I think you can see
that increased awareness

01:19:15.167 --> 01:19:17.490
from a variety of settings.

01:19:17.490 --> 01:19:20.580
But you can also see what
people are looking at

01:19:20.580 --> 01:19:23.820
in terms of information
that they're seeking.

01:19:23.820 --> 01:19:28.560
And in 2023, ADHD was the
second most viewed condition

01:19:28.560 --> 01:19:30.120
on the NHS website.

01:19:30.120 --> 01:19:33.390
There were 4.3 million page views,

01:19:33.390 --> 01:19:37.800
which I think tells us
something about the awareness

01:19:37.800 --> 01:19:40.323
and the importance of
this issue in society.

01:19:41.640 --> 01:19:43.560
That's possibly also been exacerbated

01:19:43.560 --> 01:19:45.689
by some of the medication
supply challenges

01:19:45.689 --> 01:19:49.710
that have been experienced, of course.

01:19:49.710 --> 01:19:53.040
But we have been learning from the work

01:19:53.040 --> 01:19:56.070
that regions have been
doing about the pressure

01:19:56.070 --> 01:19:57.600
that providers have been reporting.

01:19:57.600 --> 01:19:59.310
And I think it's fair
to say that that exists

01:19:59.310 --> 01:20:02.343
across the country and
it's really important

01:20:02.343 --> 01:20:05.700
that as far as possible the
NHS is able to put in place

01:20:05.700 --> 01:20:09.303
the infrastructure and the
range of that people need.

01:20:11.250 --> 01:20:13.740
As a consequence of
that, back in December,

01:20:13.740 --> 01:20:16.830
Amanda asked us to bring together people

01:20:16.830 --> 01:20:20.460
to consider the issues relating to ADHD,

01:20:20.460 --> 01:20:22.590
not to solve the problem immediately,

01:20:22.590 --> 01:20:24.570
but to try and understand
what was happening

01:20:24.570 --> 01:20:28.620
and to lay a path for what
particular improvements

01:20:28.620 --> 01:20:31.020
and interventions might
look like in the future.

01:20:31.920 --> 01:20:33.217
That's include a focus on understanding

01:20:33.217 --> 01:20:35.416
the data that we have.

01:20:35.416 --> 01:20:38.220
It's had a focus on the use

01:20:38.220 --> 01:20:41.043
and the role of
therapeutics or medication.

01:20:42.150 --> 01:20:45.600
We've tried to understand what's
driving some of the growth

01:20:45.600 --> 01:20:48.660
and the variation that we see in access

01:20:48.660 --> 01:20:51.990
and outcomes across different populations

01:20:51.990 --> 01:20:55.830
and also how well services are joined up

01:20:55.830 --> 01:20:59.403
across health education
and justice in particular.

01:21:00.840 --> 01:21:03.060
Claire's going to pick
this up in a second,

01:21:03.060 --> 01:21:06.270
but Claire has chaired a
clinical reference group

01:21:06.270 --> 01:21:09.000
which has had very strong engagement

01:21:09.000 --> 01:21:13.173
and has explored five areas
that are set out in the paper.

01:21:15.120 --> 01:21:19.080
And the paper sets out
some of the key findings

01:21:19.080 --> 01:21:20.823
and some of the next steps.

01:21:21.990 --> 01:21:23.283
I think it's important to say

01:21:23.283 --> 01:21:25.320
that in addition to some of the challenges

01:21:25.320 --> 01:21:27.720
that are clearly highlighted in the paper

01:21:27.720 --> 01:21:30.120
and through the work that's been done.

01:21:30.120 --> 01:21:31.380
There are many services

01:21:31.380 --> 01:21:34.320
and many places that are
doing really good work.

01:21:34.320 --> 01:21:36.053
And I want to just, there are of course,

01:21:36.053 --> 01:21:38.040
there's now too many to mention,

01:21:38.040 --> 01:21:40.350
but I want to just particularly call out

01:21:40.350 --> 01:21:42.216
Derbyshire and Hertfordshire,

01:21:42.216 --> 01:21:46.560
and again, those areas
have been heavily involved

01:21:46.560 --> 01:21:48.210
and influenced some of this work.

01:21:48.210 --> 01:21:50.880
And there's a kind of particular thank you

01:21:50.880 --> 01:21:53.640
to Ed Knowles and Elliot Howard-Jones,

01:21:53.640 --> 01:21:55.890
two colleagues in the West Hertfordshire

01:21:55.890 --> 01:21:58.950
and West Essex system

01:21:58.950 --> 01:22:02.070
who have been doing some
really innovative thinking

01:22:02.070 --> 01:22:06.570
and work across their system
in support of improving

01:22:06.570 --> 01:22:09.660
access for people living with ADHD.

01:22:09.660 --> 01:22:12.699
We spent a bit of time with
them earlier this week,

01:22:12.699 --> 01:22:16.680
and their model includes
a single front door triage

01:22:16.680 --> 01:22:19.500
and then simple pathways
for onward referral.

01:22:19.500 --> 01:22:22.470
It includes support
while people are waiting

01:22:22.470 --> 01:22:25.680
and that is done in partnership
with the voluntary sector

01:22:25.680 --> 01:22:28.023
and families with lived experience.

01:22:29.340 --> 01:22:31.503
And so we think there's some
really interesting things

01:22:31.503 --> 01:22:33.780
that are already happening in the NHS

01:22:33.780 --> 01:22:38.103
that can be explored further
in the second phase of work.

01:22:39.750 --> 01:22:42.810
As I said, this is a
step in many respects,

01:22:42.810 --> 01:22:44.280
the findings are an important step.

01:22:44.280 --> 01:22:46.110
They're not the full answer.

01:22:46.110 --> 01:22:48.510
And what we need to do now is test

01:22:48.510 --> 01:22:50.820
and develop some of that thinking

01:22:50.820 --> 01:22:54.180
and the potential
solutions with stakeholders

01:22:54.180 --> 01:22:56.670
and importantly with people living

01:22:56.670 --> 01:22:59.190
with ADHD and their families.

01:22:59.190 --> 01:23:01.560
And it's really important
that we do this cross sector,

01:23:01.560 --> 01:23:05.340
which is exactly what the Hertfordshire

01:23:05.340 --> 01:23:07.020
and West Essex system have done.

01:23:07.020 --> 01:23:09.510
Their work has not been
done just within the NHS.

01:23:09.510 --> 01:23:10.950
It's been done in partnership with

01:23:10.950 --> 01:23:12.900
the voluntary and community sector,

01:23:12.900 --> 01:23:15.500
with the education sector
and with local government.

01:23:16.590 --> 01:23:19.200
And so as you'll see in the paper,

01:23:19.200 --> 01:23:22.560
what we are proposing to
do is build on the work

01:23:22.560 --> 01:23:26.130
that we've done so far and
to take forward the findings

01:23:26.130 --> 01:23:28.920
to set up a cross sector task force

01:23:28.920 --> 01:23:31.620
that will bring together the NHS,

01:23:31.620 --> 01:23:33.360
our wider system partners,

01:23:33.360 --> 01:23:36.633
including local government, schools,

01:23:36.633 --> 01:23:38.490
clinicians from the NHS,

01:23:38.490 --> 01:23:40.560
the voluntary sector and beyond,

01:23:40.560 --> 01:23:43.080
so that we end up with a really joined up

01:23:43.080 --> 01:23:45.900
piece of work that is
particularly important

01:23:45.900 --> 01:23:48.540
as it says in the paper for ADHD,

01:23:48.540 --> 01:23:53.253
as the solutions don't lie
within the gift of the NHS alone.

01:23:54.090 --> 01:23:58.620
So we hope that that will lead
in the not too distant future

01:23:58.620 --> 01:24:01.980
to a bit of a turning point
for people living with ADHD

01:24:01.980 --> 01:24:03.568
who are currently trying to navigate

01:24:03.568 --> 01:24:08.568
quite a complex system in seeking support.

01:24:08.970 --> 01:24:10.860
So Chair, that's all I was going to say.

01:24:10.860 --> 01:24:11.693
I hope that makes sense.

01:24:11.693 --> 01:24:13.157
I'm going to hand to Claire
who's going to talk about

01:24:13.157 --> 01:24:16.200
the work of the CRG and
Catherine on next steps.

01:24:16.200 --> 01:24:18.690
- As you do that just to say,
I'm sure the board's seen it,

01:24:18.690 --> 01:24:21.150
what we're asked to do
is to note essentially

01:24:21.150 --> 01:24:23.100
the work undertaken today,

01:24:23.100 --> 01:24:24.360
but it's actually to endorse actually

01:24:24.360 --> 01:24:25.260
what you were just talking about,

01:24:25.260 --> 01:24:27.210
which is the next steps around task force-

01:24:27.210 --> 01:24:28.230
- Yeah.
- A multidisciplinary

01:24:28.230 --> 01:24:30.930
task force effectively, but okay, Claire.

01:24:30.930 --> 01:24:32.595
- Thank you, Chair.

01:24:32.595 --> 01:24:33.428
So I'm Claire Fuller.

01:24:33.428 --> 01:24:34.261
I'm a GP,

01:24:34.261 --> 01:24:36.360
and I'm the primary care medical
director for NHS England.

01:24:36.360 --> 01:24:38.100
So I just want to start by
saying thank you to Amanda

01:24:38.100 --> 01:24:40.740
and to Steve because it's been

01:24:40.740 --> 01:24:45.347
an absolute privilege
working on this project.

01:24:45.347 --> 01:24:47.910
So Steve and Amanda asked Claire Murdoch

01:24:47.910 --> 01:24:50.700
and myself to take the lead
operationally and clinically

01:24:50.700 --> 01:24:52.170
and then we've had the
absolute joy of working

01:24:52.170 --> 01:24:53.640
with Catherine here,

01:24:53.640 --> 01:24:56.910
and actually the energy
that it has unlocked,

01:24:56.910 --> 01:24:59.580
enabling us to take the time
to actually look at the issues

01:24:59.580 --> 01:25:01.920
and understand what is
going on has been the thing

01:25:01.920 --> 01:25:03.900
I think that will make
this a really important

01:25:03.900 --> 01:25:05.493
piece of work for us to do.

01:25:06.441 --> 01:25:08.460
So I set up the clinical reference group.

01:25:08.460 --> 01:25:10.320
40 clinicians came together,

01:25:10.320 --> 01:25:13.110
they were multi-professional,
multispecialism,

01:25:13.110 --> 01:25:17.460
and we met and for a total of
20 hours twice as a big group

01:25:17.460 --> 01:25:18.930
and then in much smaller groups

01:25:18.930 --> 01:25:21.570
having several hour long conversations

01:25:21.570 --> 01:25:24.870
in real depth about the
topics as Steve outlined.

01:25:24.870 --> 01:25:25.950
So looking at diagnosis,

01:25:25.950 --> 01:25:28.680
looking at neurodiversity in the round.

01:25:28.680 --> 01:25:33.680
So although many autistic
people also have ADHD,

01:25:33.913 --> 01:25:37.410
there are also many people that have ADHD

01:25:37.410 --> 01:25:41.280
as a single entity that
we need to make sure

01:25:41.280 --> 01:25:43.297
that we look after people
in the way that works best.

01:25:43.297 --> 01:25:45.840
We looked a lot at the
prescribing interventions,

01:25:45.840 --> 01:25:48.450
we also looked at those
interventions that are supported

01:25:48.450 --> 01:25:49.800
and do not involve medication

01:25:49.800 --> 01:25:51.870
that I don't think we necessarily

01:25:51.870 --> 01:25:55.323
have paid enough attention to previously.

01:25:56.160 --> 01:25:57.810
Two of the things that were
particularly interesting

01:25:57.810 --> 01:25:59.820
was actually the role
of health inequalities,

01:25:59.820 --> 01:26:02.580
and the fact that the
population within our prisons

01:26:02.580 --> 01:26:04.290
are more likely to have ADHD

01:26:04.290 --> 01:26:06.210
than those people not in prison,

01:26:06.210 --> 01:26:08.970
which I think is quite shocking as well.

01:26:08.970 --> 01:26:11.430
And also the impact of
adverse childhood events

01:26:11.430 --> 01:26:13.473
and trauma upon the number of people

01:26:13.473 --> 01:26:15.720
that actually present with ADHD.

01:26:15.720 --> 01:26:18.750
We also talked quite a
lot about secondary gain.

01:26:18.750 --> 01:26:22.200
So ADHD is an unusual diagnosis.

01:26:22.200 --> 01:26:25.500
So as a GP it is a diagnosis
that people often come

01:26:25.500 --> 01:26:28.380
and will knock on the door
and will seek the diagnosis.

01:26:28.380 --> 01:26:30.960
That doesn't happen with many conditions

01:26:30.960 --> 01:26:32.370
and it's spending a little
bit of time actually

01:26:32.370 --> 01:26:34.890
understanding why that's happening

01:26:34.890 --> 01:26:37.200
and why that's happening
more at the moment.

01:26:37.200 --> 01:26:40.740
So from the groups that met three themes

01:26:40.740 --> 01:26:42.720
came out in almost every conversation

01:26:42.720 --> 01:26:43.710
regardless of what title

01:26:43.710 --> 01:26:45.150
we put at the top of the conversation,

01:26:45.150 --> 01:26:46.710
which I think is always quite telling

01:26:46.710 --> 01:26:47.970
that you're having the right conversation.

01:26:47.970 --> 01:26:49.694
So we talked a lot about the right person,

01:26:49.694 --> 01:26:52.200
the right pathway, and the right support.

01:26:52.200 --> 01:26:54.540
So this is again the role
of health inequalities

01:26:54.540 --> 01:26:57.150
and actually making
sure that we are getting

01:26:57.150 --> 01:26:59.070
the right support to the right people.

01:26:59.070 --> 01:27:03.150
We find that people are
actually on multiple pathways

01:27:03.150 --> 01:27:05.460
rather than actually
receiving personalised care

01:27:05.460 --> 01:27:06.667
in a way that works for them.

01:27:06.667 --> 01:27:08.680
And also we found that people

01:27:09.570 --> 01:27:10.851
in terms of the right support,

01:27:10.851 --> 01:27:13.770
often the diagnosis was
viewed as the end point

01:27:13.770 --> 01:27:15.270
rather than actually something that

01:27:15.270 --> 01:27:18.660
should enable people to receive
the support that they need.

01:27:18.660 --> 01:27:20.520
We kept talking about converging spaces

01:27:20.520 --> 01:27:23.910
and Steve's very much
talked to this already,

01:27:23.910 --> 01:27:26.070
it's around the need
that this isn't something

01:27:26.070 --> 01:27:28.750
that the NHS can or should
try to solve on its own.

01:27:28.750 --> 01:27:31.727
Every conversation talked
about the role of education,

01:27:31.727 --> 01:27:34.170
the role of the health and justice system,

01:27:34.170 --> 01:27:38.820
the role of work and the
role of families as well.

01:27:38.820 --> 01:27:40.320
And then we talked about
the paradox of care.

01:27:40.320 --> 01:27:41.700
So this is one of those things

01:27:41.700 --> 01:27:43.470
that actually having more contact

01:27:43.470 --> 01:27:45.630
isn't necessarily making
things better for people.

01:27:45.630 --> 01:27:47.790
So I was in the surgery last Friday

01:27:47.790 --> 01:27:51.180
and coincidentally my very
first patient that came in

01:27:51.180 --> 01:27:53.490
was the mother of 14-year-old girl

01:27:53.490 --> 01:27:55.680
who's been excluded from school.

01:27:55.680 --> 01:27:59.400
She had been referred by her
school into local services.

01:27:59.400 --> 01:28:02.100
Her mother had made a self-referral

01:28:02.100 --> 01:28:03.810
and she'd also been seen by the child

01:28:03.810 --> 01:28:05.190
and adolescent mental health services

01:28:05.190 --> 01:28:06.810
who had also made a referral

01:28:06.810 --> 01:28:08.851
and her mother was coming in to me to ask

01:28:08.851 --> 01:28:11.190
for another referral,

01:28:11.190 --> 01:28:12.932
which you can see the frustration

01:28:12.932 --> 01:28:15.870
that is building for both individuals

01:28:15.870 --> 01:28:18.330
and the impact on this young person's life

01:28:18.330 --> 01:28:21.930
in terms of not receiving
the appropriate support,

01:28:21.930 --> 01:28:23.970
but instead of obviously
doing a fourth referral

01:28:23.970 --> 01:28:25.020
for this young person,

01:28:25.020 --> 01:28:28.680
we instead phoned up the
mental health trust involved

01:28:28.680 --> 01:28:29.513
and worked through

01:28:29.513 --> 01:28:31.320
what we might be able to
do in a different way.

01:28:31.320 --> 01:28:32.850
So I'm going to hand over to Catherine

01:28:32.850 --> 01:28:35.190
who'll talk through the next steps for us.

01:28:35.190 --> 01:28:36.300
- Thank you, Claire.

01:28:36.300 --> 01:28:39.660
I also wanted to start by saying
what an absolute privilege

01:28:39.660 --> 01:28:41.940
and pleasure it's been
to lead on this work

01:28:41.940 --> 01:28:43.980
over the last few months.

01:28:43.980 --> 01:28:45.390
Working with system leaders,

01:28:45.390 --> 01:28:48.120
clinical experts like you, Claire,

01:28:48.120 --> 01:28:50.610
and teams from across NHS England.

01:28:50.610 --> 01:28:53.544
The energy enthusiasm
from everyone involved

01:28:53.544 --> 01:28:57.210
to make a difference to the
lives of those living with

01:28:57.210 --> 01:28:59.430
or seeking treatment for ADHD

01:28:59.430 --> 01:29:01.080
has really been quite extraordinary

01:29:01.080 --> 01:29:03.063
and something I've not seen before.

01:29:04.230 --> 01:29:06.270
So Claire talked about the work

01:29:06.270 --> 01:29:07.470
of the clinical reference group.

01:29:07.470 --> 01:29:09.450
We also gathered system leaders

01:29:09.450 --> 01:29:11.580
and experts across NHS England

01:29:11.580 --> 01:29:15.120
and we also spent many
hours sharing expertise

01:29:15.120 --> 01:29:17.670
and discussing and analysing it.

01:29:17.670 --> 01:29:20.250
And the overarching conclusion was clear,

01:29:20.250 --> 01:29:22.620
which is the ADHD service provision

01:29:22.620 --> 01:29:26.250
and interrelated policies
need joined up approach

01:29:26.250 --> 01:29:30.600
across healthcare education
and the justice system.

01:29:30.600 --> 01:29:32.820
And there was an
overwhelming desire expressed

01:29:32.820 --> 01:29:35.670
for the issues faced by those living with

01:29:35.670 --> 01:29:37.470
or seeking treatment for ADHD

01:29:37.470 --> 01:29:42.210
to be considered in a
holistic person-centered way.

01:29:42.210 --> 01:29:45.390
And the task force that
we want to establish

01:29:45.390 --> 01:29:48.360
is going to be an important
framework for doing that.

01:29:48.360 --> 01:29:51.274
And critical to its success is going to be

01:29:51.274 --> 01:29:55.050
a programme of engagement
with people in communities

01:29:55.050 --> 01:29:56.940
that will underpin it.

01:29:56.940 --> 01:30:00.480
So we can now move quickly
to appoint chairs and members

01:30:00.480 --> 01:30:03.753
and agree in terms of reference
and get that work moving.

01:30:04.650 --> 01:30:07.320
But alongside the work of the task force,

01:30:07.320 --> 01:30:08.760
NHS England is going to use

01:30:08.760 --> 01:30:10.950
the findings that the paper sets out

01:30:10.950 --> 01:30:13.620
and that Claire has just
shared to do what we can

01:30:13.620 --> 01:30:16.053
to help systems and patients now.

01:30:17.310 --> 01:30:21.000
We've already started our
national data improvement work

01:30:21.000 --> 01:30:22.470
because we know that it's critical

01:30:22.470 --> 01:30:24.600
to understand more
about the people seeking

01:30:24.600 --> 01:30:26.130
and receiving treatment,

01:30:26.130 --> 01:30:28.497
how long they're waiting to access it

01:30:28.497 --> 01:30:31.230
and what treatment it is
that they are receiving.

01:30:31.230 --> 01:30:32.760
And then to be able to monitor

01:30:32.760 --> 01:30:35.103
the impact of changes
that we intend to make.

01:30:36.780 --> 01:30:38.040
And given the evidence

01:30:38.040 --> 01:30:41.070
that we have about services
not keeping pace with need,

01:30:41.070 --> 01:30:43.650
we need to better understand the NHS

01:30:43.650 --> 01:30:48.030
and independent sector provider
and commissioning landscape.

01:30:48.030 --> 01:30:48.990
So we're going to undertake

01:30:48.990 --> 01:30:52.410
some detailed mapping of
the provider landscape.

01:30:52.410 --> 01:30:54.120
We're going to engage with providers

01:30:54.120 --> 01:30:56.250
and analyse their outcomes

01:30:56.250 --> 01:30:57.720
and we're going to do this in partnership

01:30:57.720 --> 01:31:00.450
with stakeholders and systems.

01:31:00.450 --> 01:31:02.670
But again, we've already
started this work,

01:31:02.670 --> 01:31:06.810
and Steve has set out some
of the really fantastic work

01:31:06.810 --> 01:31:08.460
that we've seen in Hertfordshire,

01:31:08.460 --> 01:31:10.173
in Derbyshire in particular.

01:31:11.460 --> 01:31:15.510
We know that the practise
in Derbyshire in particular

01:31:15.510 --> 01:31:18.210
has been brilliant in
terms of an all-age pathway

01:31:18.210 --> 01:31:20.130
that they have developed.

01:31:20.130 --> 01:31:22.200
And we're going to share
all of the good practise

01:31:22.200 --> 01:31:25.890
that we are uncovering
nationally and across the system.

01:31:25.890 --> 01:31:27.484
And we've also started to work with Vin

01:31:27.484 --> 01:31:29.820
and his team to think about how tech

01:31:29.820 --> 01:31:32.073
can help to improve services as well.

01:31:33.240 --> 01:31:35.610
So there's a lot to do.

01:31:35.610 --> 01:31:37.110
It's really exciting

01:31:37.110 --> 01:31:39.870
and we're all committed
to getting it right.

01:31:39.870 --> 01:31:43.140
But if I may, I am going to
leave you with some words

01:31:43.140 --> 01:31:46.443
that one of our ADHD
programme team has given me.

01:31:47.880 --> 01:31:49.650
"As a colleague at NHS England

01:31:49.650 --> 01:31:52.020
who has lived experience
of neurodivergence

01:31:52.020 --> 01:31:54.210
both autism and ADHD,

01:31:54.210 --> 01:31:57.030
it's immensely encouraging to see, hear,

01:31:57.030 --> 01:32:00.480
and feel the passion and
drive to improve the care,

01:32:00.480 --> 01:32:04.170
support and outcomes for people with ADHD.

01:32:04.170 --> 01:32:05.760
This phase of work has done much

01:32:05.760 --> 01:32:09.090
to recognise the challenges in the system

01:32:09.090 --> 01:32:11.160
to accept that some of those challenges

01:32:11.160 --> 01:32:14.040
are the facts of the way
things have been done,

01:32:14.040 --> 01:32:16.017
and also highlight the commitment to work

01:32:16.017 --> 01:32:19.410
collaboratively to make things better."

01:32:19.410 --> 01:32:20.243
- Catherine, thank you.

01:32:20.243 --> 01:32:22.710
I'm going to open up for comments
actually 'cause we've got.

01:32:23.640 --> 01:32:25.403
Simon, I'm going to go to you Simon first

01:32:25.403 --> 01:32:27.690
and see if you have any comments.

01:32:27.690 --> 01:32:30.313
Probably, you are fairly
obvious target to go to,

01:32:30.313 --> 01:32:31.920
I think in terms of commentary first,

01:32:31.920 --> 01:32:33.780
but also Helen, I know Helen will come,

01:32:33.780 --> 01:32:35.697
also Navina and others around the table.

01:32:35.697 --> 01:32:37.260
But Simon, would you like to comment?

01:32:37.260 --> 01:32:38.460
- Yeah, it's a tricky one.

01:32:38.460 --> 01:32:40.260
I mean ADHD isn't new.

01:32:40.260 --> 01:32:41.783
You might be forgiven that you think

01:32:41.783 --> 01:32:44.460
that it might be from the
publicity of what it isn't.

01:32:44.460 --> 01:32:47.400
It's been around for at
least three or four decades,

01:32:47.400 --> 01:32:49.740
largely in children where
it's well-recognized,

01:32:49.740 --> 01:32:53.040
the prevalence is around 2% in the UK,

01:32:53.040 --> 01:32:54.487
and I should by the way,

01:32:54.487 --> 01:32:57.000
one group you should mention
is our group at Kings

01:32:57.000 --> 01:32:58.634
who gave you a systematic review in hours,

01:33:01.020 --> 01:33:02.970
four days anyway, four days.

01:33:02.970 --> 01:33:06.240
And the number of children diagnosed

01:33:06.240 --> 01:33:09.750
and known with ADHD has always
been under 2% suggesting

01:33:09.750 --> 01:33:12.090
that we don't have a
problem of overdiagnosis

01:33:12.090 --> 01:33:14.250
and there might be a
problem of underdiagnosis.

01:33:14.250 --> 01:33:15.900
Now I say that because in America,

01:33:15.900 --> 01:33:17.550
which has a similar prevalence,

01:33:17.550 --> 01:33:19.440
the number of children
receiving stimulants

01:33:19.440 --> 01:33:21.930
and the treatment for ADHD
is one of the treatments,

01:33:21.930 --> 01:33:24.870
the drug treatment is stimulants is 15%,

01:33:24.870 --> 01:33:29.470
suggesting that they have a
major over diagnostic issue.

01:33:32.070 --> 01:33:35.880
One reason in the UK treatment is largely

01:33:35.880 --> 01:33:37.980
given for behavioural
problems, in America,

01:33:37.980 --> 01:33:41.670
it's given to improve
academic performance,

01:33:41.670 --> 01:33:43.380
which are really very different things.

01:33:43.380 --> 01:33:46.290
And so there's a big issue in America.

01:33:46.290 --> 01:33:49.413
In the UK, it's traditionally
not been been that.

01:33:50.550 --> 01:33:54.180
It's usually thought that it
gets better as you get older.

01:33:54.180 --> 01:33:56.400
And what has changed in recent years

01:33:56.400 --> 01:33:59.370
has been more and more
emphasis on adult ADHD

01:33:59.370 --> 01:34:02.520
which is a much harder
to categorise condition.

01:34:02.520 --> 01:34:06.570
And that is the one that has
shown a remarkable increase

01:34:06.570 --> 01:34:10.680
in media, in presentation, in demand,

01:34:10.680 --> 01:34:13.319
in a really very short space of time.

01:34:13.319 --> 01:34:17.760
It's unlikely that a change on that scale

01:34:17.760 --> 01:34:22.170
is simply due to better
recognition or help seeking

01:34:22.170 --> 01:34:24.360
and it suggests that there's a degree

01:34:24.360 --> 01:34:27.930
of diagnostic creep going on,

01:34:27.930 --> 01:34:32.930
or reevaluation of feelings,
symptoms, emotions, et cetera.

01:34:34.170 --> 01:34:35.970
So it's a complicated issue.

01:34:35.970 --> 01:34:38.397
It's happening very fast
as I'm sure you know.

01:34:38.397 --> 01:34:42.330
And the problem has
been in CAMHS services,

01:34:42.330 --> 01:34:44.280
we've still despite the investment,

01:34:44.280 --> 01:34:46.620
don't have sufficient people in CAMHS.

01:34:46.620 --> 01:34:47.850
And the main problem it has

01:34:47.850 --> 01:34:50.040
for the last few years
has been waiting lists.

01:34:50.040 --> 01:34:53.100
And that's important because
to get a diagnosis of ADHD,

01:34:53.100 --> 01:34:54.390
it is absolutely essential

01:34:54.390 --> 01:34:57.360
you have a multidisciplinary assessment

01:34:57.360 --> 01:34:59.160
and because there are many other issues

01:34:59.160 --> 01:35:00.030
that could be going on,

01:35:00.030 --> 01:35:02.850
child psychiatry and
psychology is complicated

01:35:02.850 --> 01:35:05.070
and with a good
multidisciplinary assessment

01:35:05.070 --> 01:35:07.620
you can get a good reliable diagnosis

01:35:07.620 --> 01:35:09.660
and lead on to management and treatment,

01:35:09.660 --> 01:35:11.358
that's failing at the moment.

01:35:11.358 --> 01:35:14.190
A lot of people are waiting too long

01:35:14.190 --> 01:35:17.190
and are being diverted
into private practise

01:35:17.190 --> 01:35:18.630
and private providers,

01:35:18.630 --> 01:35:21.188
some of whom as you saw
that Panorama programme,

01:35:21.188 --> 01:35:22.262
are less than,

01:35:22.262 --> 01:35:24.210
well, how should I put it not,

01:35:24.210 --> 01:35:26.850
do not have the standards
that we would expect

01:35:26.850 --> 01:35:29.700
and seem to make the
diagnosis incredibly easy

01:35:29.700 --> 01:35:32.340
and certainly don't work
in multidisciplinary teams

01:35:32.340 --> 01:35:35.187
and then push back into
primary care of people now

01:35:35.187 --> 01:35:38.430
who have a label which
may or may not be correct.

01:35:38.430 --> 01:35:41.010
And that's where some of the problems

01:35:41.010 --> 01:35:43.110
we're seeing at the
moment are coming from.

01:35:43.110 --> 01:35:46.470
But I would say that when you
see this remarkable change

01:35:46.470 --> 01:35:48.300
in a very short period of time,

01:35:48.300 --> 01:35:50.250
you need to think also not just about

01:35:50.250 --> 01:35:52.980
increased awareness, help seeking,

01:35:52.980 --> 01:35:55.200
but also about cultural
and social factors.

01:35:55.200 --> 01:35:56.880
So it's good that you're taking this

01:35:56.880 --> 01:35:58.280
very seriously and properly.

01:35:59.220 --> 01:36:01.710
I think the issues in
adults are not the same

01:36:01.710 --> 01:36:05.040
as issues in children and
actually rather more difficult

01:36:05.040 --> 01:36:08.130
and just remember that
the medication prescribed

01:36:08.130 --> 01:36:11.520
is a class two drug and it is
not without its side effects.

01:36:11.520 --> 01:36:13.353
In fact, they can be quite severe.

01:36:14.625 --> 01:36:17.100
And we need to think about that as well,

01:36:17.100 --> 01:36:19.803
which is why we need to
proceed with caution.

01:36:20.730 --> 01:36:22.050
- Simon, thank you.

01:36:22.050 --> 01:36:23.610
Helen, would you like to comment?

01:36:23.610 --> 01:36:25.080
- Just briefly.

01:36:25.080 --> 01:36:27.030
I was absolutely like to
see this on the agenda

01:36:27.030 --> 01:36:29.460
because you know, like you
Claire, I'm a frontline GP,

01:36:29.460 --> 01:36:31.830
and this is something that
we've seen a huge upswing

01:36:31.830 --> 01:36:34.980
in people seeking help
with, particularly adults.

01:36:34.980 --> 01:36:37.470
So children, young people
has been a constant

01:36:37.470 --> 01:36:38.760
drumbeat throughout my profession

01:36:38.760 --> 01:36:40.980
for many decades in practise.

01:36:40.980 --> 01:36:43.110
But actually, it's the
adults coming forward

01:36:43.110 --> 01:36:45.600
and for many people my perception,

01:36:45.600 --> 01:36:47.940
and I'm really curious to see
what comes out of your work

01:36:47.940 --> 01:36:49.110
is that what many people want

01:36:49.110 --> 01:36:50.850
is to understand themselves better

01:36:50.850 --> 01:36:52.784
so that they can carry
on living their lives.

01:36:52.784 --> 01:36:55.050
They've known that in some way

01:36:55.050 --> 01:36:56.736
they are slightly
different from their peers

01:36:56.736 --> 01:36:58.230
or their work colleagues.

01:36:58.230 --> 01:36:59.360
And a lot of people come to me

01:36:59.360 --> 01:37:01.140
when they're having HR difficulties

01:37:01.140 --> 01:37:02.490
or they're having trouble at work

01:37:02.490 --> 01:37:03.630
or trouble with relationships

01:37:03.630 --> 01:37:04.560
and trying to make sense of it.

01:37:04.560 --> 01:37:06.540
And I think the media upswing,

01:37:06.540 --> 01:37:08.190
I think as you alluded to, Richard,

01:37:08.190 --> 01:37:10.410
has brought a huge amount of awareness

01:37:10.410 --> 01:37:12.440
and people questioning of self,

01:37:12.440 --> 01:37:14.490
whereas, of course, in younger people,

01:37:14.490 --> 01:37:17.517
you mentioned Simon about
school performance and so on.

01:37:17.517 --> 01:37:19.320
And in a more disruptive sense,

01:37:19.320 --> 01:37:21.150
people needing something more urgent.

01:37:21.150 --> 01:37:23.040
And so if we can disentangle it,

01:37:23.040 --> 01:37:24.030
we can help people

01:37:24.030 --> 01:37:26.910
and work out what the NHS
should and could be doing

01:37:26.910 --> 01:37:29.430
and work out what other agencies
could and should be doing.

01:37:29.430 --> 01:37:31.920
And if we can then inform wider society

01:37:31.920 --> 01:37:33.630
and cross government approaches,

01:37:33.630 --> 01:37:34.890
I think that will be a huge gift

01:37:34.890 --> 01:37:36.480
from the NHS to the wider system.

01:37:36.480 --> 01:37:37.860
So thank you.

01:37:37.860 --> 01:37:41.580
I'm personally delighted
and full support from me,

01:37:41.580 --> 01:37:43.890
but thanks for doing the
work you've done so far.

01:37:43.890 --> 01:37:45.037
Thanks.
- Thanks Helen.

01:37:45.037 --> 01:37:46.370
Thank you very much.

01:37:46.370 --> 01:37:48.491
Any other comments or reflections?

01:37:48.491 --> 01:37:49.324
Amanda, do you want to?

01:37:49.324 --> 01:37:51.082
David, sorry.

01:37:51.082 --> 01:37:51.934
- So thank you,

01:37:51.934 --> 01:37:54.600
and Simon, thanks for the masterclass

01:37:54.600 --> 01:37:58.890
in a soundbite as well.

01:37:58.890 --> 01:38:00.630
I think to the point that for children,

01:38:00.630 --> 01:38:03.510
this has been around for a long time,

01:38:03.510 --> 01:38:05.670
in fact, I think pretty
much all of my career

01:38:05.670 --> 01:38:07.751
and as a director of social services,

01:38:07.751 --> 01:38:11.490
this was one of the key issues
that people were presenting

01:38:11.490 --> 01:38:14.670
because in order to get
access to special education,

01:38:14.670 --> 01:38:18.210
you need an assessment and
a statement to get that.

01:38:18.210 --> 01:38:21.060
Actually, Claire, your story about

01:38:21.060 --> 01:38:23.220
the mum that came into
your surgery last Friday,

01:38:23.220 --> 01:38:25.230
for me is very typical.

01:38:25.230 --> 01:38:27.690
These are kids who are
often suspended from school,

01:38:27.690 --> 01:38:30.360
not in school from the
poorer families, et cetera.

01:38:30.360 --> 01:38:34.560
So actually, the emphasis
around inter-agency working

01:38:34.560 --> 01:38:36.810
and getting the buy-in of both education

01:38:36.810 --> 01:38:39.960
and local government is going
to be absolutely essential.

01:38:39.960 --> 01:38:41.610
And this issue about being clear

01:38:41.610 --> 01:38:45.681
about the respective roles
that the different agencies

01:38:45.681 --> 01:38:50.681
and professionals play, I
do think is is critical.

01:38:51.150 --> 01:38:53.040
And this week on the radio,

01:38:53.040 --> 01:38:55.620
we've had the local
government association saying

01:38:55.620 --> 01:38:59.460
the fastest rising overspending
budget in local government

01:38:59.460 --> 01:39:02.700
is the home to school transport budget.

01:39:02.700 --> 01:39:03.963
These kids,

01:39:05.760 --> 01:39:07.110
they're the same kids.

01:39:07.110 --> 01:39:09.060
- And just to make one last point,

01:39:09.060 --> 01:39:11.518
I mean it is sometimes
easy to be sceptical

01:39:11.518 --> 01:39:13.110
about some of these diagnoses,

01:39:13.110 --> 01:39:15.390
but myself and Alana Singer
wrote a paper in the Lancet

01:39:15.390 --> 01:39:17.490
four or five years ago looking at,

01:39:17.490 --> 01:39:20.430
and people worry about giving
medication to children.

01:39:20.430 --> 01:39:21.835
That seems to be something that that's,

01:39:21.835 --> 01:39:23.850
a lot of people think
that that's kind of wrong,

01:39:23.850 --> 01:39:25.047
you just shouldn't do it.

01:39:25.047 --> 01:39:27.025
And actually, what we showed was that

01:39:27.025 --> 01:39:30.690
children who had a good
proper diagnosis of ADHD

01:39:30.690 --> 01:39:33.150
when done in a multidisciplinary way

01:39:33.150 --> 01:39:35.501
and then who were treated
with stimulant medication,

01:39:35.501 --> 01:39:39.060
they in rather, people say,
"Oh, you're drugging children."

01:39:39.060 --> 01:39:42.660
In fact, they felt much
more authentic on medication

01:39:42.660 --> 01:39:45.300
and when asked, "Which do
you think is your true self?"

01:39:45.300 --> 01:39:47.460
They said, "I'm much better
when I'm on medication."

01:39:47.460 --> 01:39:49.530
That's what I feel is the real me.

01:39:49.530 --> 01:39:51.900
When I'm off medication,
I'm a very different person

01:39:51.900 --> 01:39:54.270
and everything is chaotic, et cetera."

01:39:54.270 --> 01:39:57.060
So it's easy to be sceptical about that

01:39:57.060 --> 01:39:59.201
and I like being sceptical,

01:39:59.201 --> 01:40:02.340
but actually when properly
diagnosed with proper treatment,

01:40:02.340 --> 01:40:03.960
proper teams, et cetera,

01:40:03.960 --> 01:40:06.480
it's a very important
and useful diagnosis.

01:40:06.480 --> 01:40:07.470
- Yeah.

01:40:07.470 --> 01:40:08.310
- Thank you.

01:40:08.310 --> 01:40:09.143
Amanda.

01:40:09.143 --> 01:40:10.784
- Yeah, do you mind if I just just

01:40:10.784 --> 01:40:11.751
say one final thing at the end?

01:40:11.751 --> 01:40:13.200
So I think it's been a
brilliant conversation,

01:40:13.200 --> 01:40:16.500
but actually, I just
wanted to add, if I may,

01:40:16.500 --> 01:40:19.020
my thanks to Steve, to Claire Murdoch,

01:40:19.020 --> 01:40:20.100
who's not able to be here,

01:40:20.100 --> 01:40:23.280
as well as Claire Fuller
who is, and Catherine,

01:40:23.280 --> 01:40:26.790
because when I asked you
to pick up this work,

01:40:26.790 --> 01:40:28.052
it was because we really recognised

01:40:28.052 --> 01:40:31.320
that this was a complicated issue,

01:40:31.320 --> 01:40:32.493
but it was also something

01:40:32.493 --> 01:40:36.300
that was difficult and really mattered.

01:40:36.300 --> 01:40:38.760
And we were hearing not just
from colleagues within the NHS,

01:40:38.760 --> 01:40:40.350
but actually from really
important partners

01:40:40.350 --> 01:40:42.630
like Health Watch that this was something

01:40:42.630 --> 01:40:43.890
that was high on their agenda.

01:40:43.890 --> 01:40:48.210
And I just want to thank you
for the very thoughtful way

01:40:48.210 --> 01:40:49.460
that you've approached it

01:40:50.698 --> 01:40:53.340
and not trying to
oversimplify a complex issue

01:40:53.340 --> 01:40:56.910
as this discussion has actually
just absolutely brought out,

01:40:56.910 --> 01:41:01.230
but also in coming up with
really concrete next steps.

01:41:01.230 --> 01:41:03.450
But there also aren't closing down

01:41:03.450 --> 01:41:04.980
into a set of immediate actions.

01:41:04.980 --> 01:41:06.360
They're doing some immediate actions,

01:41:06.360 --> 01:41:07.830
