It also looks ahead to
planning for '24, '25.

01:53:30.540 --> 01:53:32.790
It summarises our priorities
for the year ahead.

01:53:32.790 --> 01:53:34.956
I'm the Accountable Emergency Officer

01:53:34.956 --> 01:53:37.080
and the National Director
for NHS Resilience

01:53:37.080 --> 01:53:40.770
is Mike Prentice, who's the
main author of the paper.

01:53:40.770 --> 01:53:45.630
And as a result of the
stretching work of the,

01:53:45.630 --> 01:53:48.450
what used to be the EPRR
team with renaming them

01:53:48.450 --> 01:53:50.640
or they have renamed themselves
the NHS Resilience team,

01:53:50.640 --> 01:53:53.070
which is reflected in the paper.

01:53:53.070 --> 01:53:54.927
We've detailed the documents
that have been published

01:53:54.927 --> 01:53:56.190
and the products produced

01:53:56.190 --> 01:53:58.882
that are already in the public domain.

01:53:58.882 --> 01:54:01.770
And given the list of incidents

01:54:01.770 --> 01:54:03.690
that have been managed by the team.

01:54:03.690 --> 01:54:05.520
It's probably worth noting
that we stepped down

01:54:05.520 --> 01:54:08.910
the formal COVID response
on the 18th of May.

01:54:08.910 --> 01:54:10.680
But as I think several people have said,

01:54:10.680 --> 01:54:12.420
we are clearly still in recovery.

01:54:12.420 --> 01:54:13.890
So although, it's no
longer a formal incident,

01:54:13.890 --> 01:54:16.177
it very much affect a lot of our work

01:54:16.177 --> 01:54:17.823
and a lot of our teams.

01:54:18.990 --> 01:54:21.870
We are continuing to learn from COVID.

01:54:21.870 --> 01:54:24.270
So what we learned and
what the team's developed

01:54:24.270 --> 01:54:26.520
is used to coordinate incident responses.

01:54:26.520 --> 01:54:29.190
And I saw that up close

01:54:29.190 --> 01:54:31.830
in the management of industrial action.

01:54:31.830 --> 01:54:34.923
That's happened since I
have been in this role.

01:54:37.110 --> 01:54:39.900
One of the major uses of
the team's time this year

01:54:39.900 --> 01:54:40.890
has, of course, been

01:54:40.890 --> 01:54:43.140
the management of continued
industrial action.

01:54:43.140 --> 01:54:44.520
- Yeah.

01:54:44.520 --> 01:54:47.640
- And I would ask the
board to sort of note

01:54:47.640 --> 01:54:48.473
the content of the report,

01:54:48.473 --> 01:54:51.750
but also obviously, say thank
you to the Resilience team

01:54:51.750 --> 01:54:53.850
and for the very extended network

01:54:53.850 --> 01:54:55.230
of people that work with them.

01:54:55.230 --> 01:54:57.780
We see the team that is based
on the sixth floor here,

01:54:57.780 --> 01:55:00.540
but obviously, there are
teams across NHS England

01:55:00.540 --> 01:55:03.723
and in systems that are
coordinating all of this work.

01:55:05.400 --> 01:55:06.233
- Very good.

01:55:06.233 --> 01:55:09.699
I mean, I think it's a
report well worth studying.

01:55:09.699 --> 01:55:13.140
I mean, COVID is a very
recent major, major incident

01:55:13.140 --> 01:55:17.670
actually, in terms of our ability
to handle and manage that.

01:55:17.670 --> 01:55:18.503
And you're quite right.

01:55:18.503 --> 01:55:20.710
You draw out the fact
that industrial action

01:55:22.974 --> 01:55:26.940
has placed us under very
real operational stresses,

01:55:26.940 --> 01:55:28.190
which we've had to manage

01:55:29.340 --> 01:55:33.300
and that's required really
strong joint working,

01:55:33.300 --> 01:55:36.810
just to demonstrate and prove
that we can remain resilient

01:55:36.810 --> 01:55:38.460
and continue to operate

01:55:38.460 --> 01:55:41.250
and perform in spite of that pressure.

01:55:41.250 --> 01:55:43.590
I did a visit to references

01:55:43.590 --> 01:55:46.320
the National Ambulance Resilience Unit

01:55:46.320 --> 01:55:48.510
and it here talks about the
West Midlands Ambulance Service

01:55:48.510 --> 01:55:50.820
and actually the contract, I think it says

01:55:50.820 --> 01:55:53.400
is given an open procurement is actually

01:55:53.400 --> 01:55:56.430
going to move the London
Ambulance Service.

01:55:56.430 --> 01:55:57.810
But actually, when I was up on a visit

01:55:57.810 --> 01:55:59.160
to the West Midlands Ambulance Service,

01:55:59.160 --> 01:56:01.560
actually, they did show me essentially

01:56:01.560 --> 01:56:03.270
the ambulance resilience unit

01:56:03.270 --> 01:56:06.389
and talk me through the nature
of the complex incidents

01:56:06.389 --> 01:56:08.790
that they would actually have to manage.

01:56:08.790 --> 01:56:11.382
And actually, they also
showed me, forgive me,

01:56:11.382 --> 01:56:14.580
it was rather a boy zone trip
of some of the kit and so on,

01:56:14.580 --> 01:56:15.810
that enables them to deal with

01:56:15.810 --> 01:56:18.219
some of those bay major incidents

01:56:18.219 --> 01:56:21.210
that you can imagine.

01:56:21.210 --> 01:56:23.047
The assurance process
is really important here

01:56:23.047 --> 01:56:25.421
and it is set out in some detail

01:56:25.421 --> 01:56:27.840
and building up actually
from local assurance

01:56:27.840 --> 01:56:28.920
through up into regional.

01:56:28.920 --> 01:56:30.780
We've got improved training.

01:56:30.780 --> 01:56:33.280
We've looked at improved
guidance that you can see

01:56:34.173 --> 01:56:36.433
and in particular around
mass casualties as

01:56:36.433 --> 01:56:40.680
a burns annex on a mass
casualty that's been improved.

01:56:40.680 --> 01:56:42.750
We can see that pandemic disease

01:56:42.750 --> 01:56:47.130
is operating framework
that has been developed

01:56:47.130 --> 01:56:50.005
and will be published.

01:56:50.005 --> 01:56:53.010
And so actually, it's a really quite good

01:56:53.010 --> 01:56:53.940
and interesting paper,

01:56:53.940 --> 01:56:57.480
but it imagines and
envisages how well the NHS

01:56:57.480 --> 01:57:02.480
can respond resiliently to
emergency and complex incidents.

01:57:02.850 --> 01:57:06.690
So we shouldn't take it
lightly and as we noted,

01:57:06.690 --> 01:57:07.523
we should note actually

01:57:07.523 --> 01:57:09.660
that it's a very important capability

01:57:09.660 --> 01:57:11.700
and going back to my loop,

01:57:11.700 --> 01:57:14.100
demonstrated really very significantly

01:57:14.100 --> 01:57:17.100
only a couple of years
ago actually with COVID.

01:57:17.100 --> 01:57:19.680
But Amanda, do you want
to comment further?

01:57:19.680 --> 01:57:20.730
- Well, thank you, Richard.

01:57:20.730 --> 01:57:22.050
I think you've summed it up very well,

01:57:22.050 --> 01:57:25.650
but Emily, I mean I think
this paper really does show,

01:57:25.650 --> 01:57:27.450
it's the sheer range of things

01:57:27.450 --> 01:57:31.530
that the team are dealing
with sort of at any one time.

01:57:31.530 --> 01:57:34.290
And so having industrial
action as a sort of constant

01:57:34.290 --> 01:57:37.380
through the year and the
ongoing impact from COVID

01:57:37.380 --> 01:57:39.750
and then all of these
individual incidents,

01:57:39.750 --> 01:57:43.680
I think we said it a lot in the pandemic,

01:57:43.680 --> 01:57:46.740
what an amazing job these teams did,

01:57:46.740 --> 01:57:48.600
and we said it a lot
during industrial action,

01:57:48.600 --> 01:57:50.596
but actually bringing it
together I think really does

01:57:50.596 --> 01:57:52.083
shine a light again on the fact

01:57:52.083 --> 01:57:54.000
that it's not just those things,

01:57:54.000 --> 01:57:57.720
it's the everyday things that
they're dealing with as well.

01:57:57.720 --> 01:58:00.873
But I guess my question
was whether or not,

01:58:02.070 --> 01:58:04.950
I can see the methodologies
changed and reassuringly,

01:58:04.950 --> 01:58:08.583
I think paragraph 37
it does say, was it 37?

01:58:09.540 --> 01:58:11.670
Yeah, no regions reported
providers that unable

01:58:11.670 --> 01:58:14.070
to respond to an emergency
or a major incident.

01:58:14.070 --> 01:58:17.580
It was just whether or not
we actually on reflection

01:58:17.580 --> 01:58:22.580
think that there is either more capacity

01:58:22.710 --> 01:58:25.680
that we need to be identifying
to support this work

01:58:25.680 --> 01:58:29.430
internally or indeed
more widely in the NHS

01:58:29.430 --> 01:58:32.040
or whether we actually
think we've learned so much

01:58:32.040 --> 01:58:34.410
from how to do things really effectively

01:58:34.410 --> 01:58:35.790
over the last few years.

01:58:35.790 --> 01:58:39.690
Partly, because we've had to learn at pace

01:58:39.690 --> 01:58:43.560
that actually we've now got
as resilient arrangements.

01:58:43.560 --> 01:58:45.600
Difficult question, sorry.

01:58:45.600 --> 01:58:48.780
- Yeah, I mean, at the risk
of sounding very boring,

01:58:48.780 --> 01:58:51.900
I think it's a both and
answer in that, I know.

01:58:51.900 --> 01:58:53.333
- You said that before actually.

01:58:53.333 --> 01:58:57.300
- Well, I know the EPR, sorry,
I'm calling the EPR team.

01:58:57.300 --> 01:59:00.810
The Resilience team has
struggled with enabling people

01:59:00.810 --> 01:59:02.850
to have annual leave this year.

01:59:02.850 --> 01:59:06.870
For example, because they've
just always been both,

01:59:06.870 --> 01:59:07.920
as you say, the low level.

01:59:07.920 --> 01:59:10.200
I actually just opened on my email,

01:59:10.200 --> 01:59:12.360
there's six incidents
that they're actually

01:59:12.360 --> 01:59:14.250
just sent me an update on today, right?

01:59:14.250 --> 01:59:16.890
So there is an ongoing level
of work all of the time

01:59:16.890 --> 01:59:20.070
and industrial action has
happened so regularly,

01:59:20.070 --> 01:59:21.723
which requires everybody to be on board

01:59:21.723 --> 01:59:25.200
that I think it's fair to say
the team's been challenged

01:59:25.200 --> 01:59:26.820
to make sure they've got the right level

01:59:26.820 --> 01:59:28.143
of resilience themselves.

01:59:29.220 --> 01:59:32.040
So we do need to look at
that in our arrangements

01:59:32.040 --> 01:59:34.503
for time off in lieu, et cetera.

01:59:35.610 --> 01:59:36.840
I think there is an and though,

01:59:36.840 --> 01:59:39.630
which is what we noticed
in industrial action,

01:59:39.630 --> 01:59:42.870
the ones that started it over in December

01:59:42.870 --> 01:59:46.257
was that the co-location
of the UEC operations team

01:59:46.257 --> 01:59:49.830
and the Resilience team meant that

01:59:49.830 --> 01:59:52.530
we had more than double
the capacity kind of thing

01:59:52.530 --> 01:59:54.330
because the insights
and the kind of people

01:59:54.330 --> 01:59:55.830
that those teams are both talking to

01:59:55.830 --> 01:59:57.180
and the better data collection

01:59:57.180 --> 01:59:59.490
that we've now got from systems

01:59:59.490 --> 02:00:01.589
has really reinforced
our ability to respond.

02:00:01.589 --> 02:00:04.410
And I would imagine the same
thing would be true at system

02:00:04.410 --> 02:00:05.790
and trust level if we
put the teams together

02:00:05.790 --> 02:00:08.970
that are managing kind of
similar things in similar ways,

02:00:08.970 --> 02:00:10.320
there probably is more resilience there

02:00:10.320 --> 02:00:12.060
than we quite spotted,

02:00:12.060 --> 02:00:13.890
but I think that takes an ability

02:00:13.890 --> 02:00:15.333
to step back and look at it.

02:00:16.976 --> 02:00:19.080
And I noted, and to your point,

02:00:19.080 --> 02:00:20.340
if everybody's busy all the time,

02:00:20.340 --> 02:00:21.453
you don't get the time to
step back and think about.

02:00:21.453 --> 02:00:24.000
Have we actually got the right
people in the right place?

02:00:24.000 --> 02:00:25.800
So I think it's a good question,

02:00:25.800 --> 02:00:26.910
which I'll take back to Mike

02:00:26.910 --> 02:00:28.080
and think about what would it take

02:00:28.080 --> 02:00:30.180
to properly look at
that level of resilience

02:00:30.180 --> 02:00:31.620
and see what we want to do to build it,

02:00:31.620 --> 02:00:33.450
particularly given the
COVID inquiry itself

02:00:33.450 --> 02:00:34.860
is also taking quite a lot of the teams.

02:00:34.860 --> 02:00:36.180
- Thank you.
- Thank you.

02:00:36.180 --> 02:00:37.410
- Jane, did you want to come in or?

02:00:37.410 --> 02:00:39.150
- Just a quick point.

02:00:39.150 --> 02:00:40.560
Thank you very much for the paper.

02:00:40.560 --> 02:00:42.300
I was particularly interested, obviously,

02:00:42.300 --> 02:00:43.800
that you've got a refresh coming through

02:00:43.800 --> 02:00:44.970
on the operating framework.

02:00:44.970 --> 02:00:47.400
- Yeah.
- Pandemic diseases,

02:00:47.400 --> 02:00:49.770
I mean it's sort of well-documented

02:00:49.770 --> 02:00:52.200
that around the world lots
of operating frameworks

02:00:52.200 --> 02:00:54.633
in lots of developed
countries were found wanting.

02:00:54.633 --> 02:00:58.290
So I just would sort of
think it'd be particularly

02:00:58.290 --> 02:01:01.680
incumbent on this board
to revisit that regularly

02:01:01.680 --> 02:01:04.350
even as it fades from memory,

02:01:04.350 --> 02:01:06.690
just to make sure that
we're really up to speed

02:01:06.690 --> 02:01:08.400
because you know, as I say,

02:01:08.400 --> 02:01:10.440
there were lots of operating frameworks

02:01:10.440 --> 02:01:13.620
that haven't been revisited
regularly as it turned out.

02:01:13.620 --> 02:01:15.870
And I think that's one of
the long term functions

02:01:15.870 --> 02:01:17.340
we can perform as a board

02:01:17.340 --> 02:01:20.654
to go back to the things
that are front of mind.

02:01:20.654 --> 02:01:21.487
- Yeah, thank you.

02:01:21.487 --> 02:01:24.240
And I'm going to bring David
in for the last comment.

02:01:24.240 --> 02:01:26.790
The one thing I would throw into the room

02:01:26.790 --> 02:01:31.450
as you would think about
climate and you think about heat

02:01:32.310 --> 02:01:34.830
and you think about our capability

02:01:34.830 --> 02:01:36.360
to operate through severe,

02:01:36.360 --> 02:01:39.330
I mean it's very significantly
different weather conditions,

02:01:39.330 --> 02:01:41.970
particularly heat, and that's in our,

02:01:41.970 --> 02:01:43.740
actually more broadly
than in our hospitals

02:01:43.740 --> 02:01:45.300
would be quite helpful.

02:01:45.300 --> 02:01:48.390
I wouldn't mind knowing
offline to what extent

02:01:48.390 --> 02:01:53.390
we are looking at and essentially
forecasting or looking,

02:01:54.090 --> 02:01:56.100
not looking forward in an emotional sense,

02:01:56.100 --> 02:01:59.670
looking forward to changes in the climate

02:01:59.670 --> 02:02:01.720
will impact on our operational capability

02:02:02.591 --> 02:02:04.530
attaching to climate actually,

02:02:04.530 --> 02:02:07.770
would be quite helpful to see
that on a forward trajectory.

02:02:07.770 --> 02:02:08.870
David, do you want to?

02:02:10.583 --> 02:02:12.630
- Yeah, I was going to mention it offline,

02:02:12.630 --> 02:02:16.020
but Amanda's question raised it

02:02:16.020 --> 02:02:18.150
and I'm not looking for an answer

02:02:18.150 --> 02:02:21.270
and I was slightly
hesitant about raising it,

02:02:21.270 --> 02:02:24.684
but the terrorism threat
must be quite high-

02:02:24.684 --> 02:02:26.012
- Yeah.
- And the realism of it-

02:02:26.012 --> 02:02:27.180
- Yeah.
- And it was

02:02:27.180 --> 02:02:30.753
amidst industrial action,
COVID disease, et cetera.

02:02:31.590 --> 02:02:33.023
The resilience of the Resilience team

02:02:33.023 --> 02:02:36.600
to deal with that kind
of shock has got to be

02:02:36.600 --> 02:02:39.120
a part and parcel of it.

02:02:39.120 --> 02:02:42.270
And again, I'm not looking
for an answer, Emily,

02:02:42.270 --> 02:02:45.660
but there's reference
into the Manchester Arena-

02:02:45.660 --> 02:02:46.681
- There is, yeah.
- And actually how

02:02:46.681 --> 02:02:49.680
that learning is incorporated

02:02:49.680 --> 02:02:53.340
and I guess the alert levels be it,

02:02:53.340 --> 02:02:56.520
so that is realistic.

02:02:56.520 --> 02:02:57.810
Vin's obviously going to say something.

02:02:57.810 --> 02:02:58.643
- You're going to come?
- I was just going to

02:02:58.643 --> 02:03:00.210
I used to work with this team

02:03:00.210 --> 02:03:02.580
in my substantive role
as medical director.

02:03:02.580 --> 02:03:06.450
Firstly, just say they do
really detailed action reviews

02:03:06.450 --> 02:03:09.450
both immediately and then
weeks and months afterwards.

02:03:09.450 --> 02:03:13.440
So as part of the learning of
the Manchester Arena bombing,

02:03:13.440 --> 02:03:17.130
so that we're putting lessons
into practise all immediately.

02:03:17.130 --> 02:03:18.203
The second point I was just going to make

02:03:18.203 --> 02:03:21.682
is that this team is
recognised internationally

02:03:21.682 --> 02:03:24.360
and I think they do learn repeatedly,

02:03:24.360 --> 02:03:25.980
but also they've had a significant

02:03:25.980 --> 02:03:27.150
influence in internationally.

02:03:27.150 --> 02:03:30.150
It's one of the things
the NHS is really good at.

02:03:30.150 --> 02:03:31.380
- Thank you, Vin.

02:03:31.380 --> 02:03:32.733
Emily, thank you very much.

02:03:33.660 --> 02:03:34.770
So I think that closes

02:03:34.770 --> 02:03:36.900
the sort of formal business of the board.

02:03:36.900 --> 02:03:37.820
So let me just go,

02:03:37.820 --> 02:03:40.863
is there any other business
anyone wants to raise?

02:03:42.540 --> 02:03:44.010
Okay, well, in that case,

02:03:44.010 --> 02:03:47.040
